tile can contain at most one S square.\n\nThus at least n-1 tiles needed to cover all S squares (since each tile can cover at most one S square). However, we need to cover all squares to the right of diagonal, not just S. But the argument says each tile can cover at most one S square, and there are n-1 S squares, so we need at least n-1 tiles. That is correct.\n\nSimilarly for T (left-of-diagonal immediate squares). So total at least 2n-2 tiles.\n\nNow the construction: R_i for i=1..n-1 covers row i columns i+1..n. L_i for i=2..n covers row i columns 1..i-1. These are indeed disjoint: each row i has at most two tiles: R_i (if i <= n-1) and L_i (if i >= 2). But they claim all tiles are disjoint across rows: R_i are in distinct rows, L_i are in distinct rows. Also R_i and L_i are in the same row i (if both exist). But they claim no two tiles share a unit square. However, R_i and L_i are in the same row i but cover disjoint column intervals: L_i covers columns 1..i-1, R_i covers columns i+1..n. So they don't overlap. So indeed each row i has at most two tiles covering all squares except the diagonal square (i,i). So total tiles = (n-1) + (n-1) = 2n-2. So the construction works.\n\nThus the answer 4048 is correct.\n\nNow we need to check reasoning validity: The solution uses valid methods: reduction via symmetry/permutation, combinatorial argument (lower bound using counting of S and T), proof by contradiction for claim about tile containing at most one S square, and explicit construction. All are valid. There's no invalid reasoning like trial and error. So Level 2 passes.\n\nNow Level 3: Presentation issues. Are there any justification gaps or critical errors? Let's examine the solution for any missing details.\n\nThe solution's lower bound claim: \"Since i+1 ≤ j, the cell (j,i+1) lies on or to the left of the diagonal; in particular, when i+1=j it equals the diagonal cell (j,j), which is uncovered.\" The argument is a bit incomplete: they need to argue that for any i<j, the rectangle containing (i,i+1) and (j,j+1) must contain (i+1,i+1) (or (j,i+1) as they said). They said \"Since i+1 ≤ j, the cell (j,i+1) lies on or to the left of the diagonal; in particular, when i+1=j it equals the diagonal cell (j,j), which is uncovered.\" This is enough to show that tile would contain an uncovered square only when i+1=j. But they need to argue that i+1=j must hold for any tile containing two S squares? Actually, if i+1 < j, then (j,i+1) is left of diagonal but not uncovered. However, the tile also contains (i+1,i+1) as we argued. They didn't mention that. They only mention (j,i+1) and the case i+1=j. So the claim that any tile containing two S squares must contain an uncovered square is not fully justified: they only consider the case i+1=j. But they could argue that if i+1 < j, then (i+1,i+1) is in the rectangle, which is uncovered. However they didn't explicitly state that. So there is a justification gap: they haven't fully justified the claim for all i<j, but the reasoning is essentially correct and can be filled in. This is a presentation issue, not a logical error. So it's a Justification Gap.\n\nSimilarly for T they say \"Exactly the same argument shows that each tile can contain at most one element of T\". That is fine.\n\nConstruction: They claim \"All tiles R_i lie in distinct rows, and all tiles L_i also lie in distinct rows; moreover a tile R_i occupies columns i+1,...,n while a tile L_i occupies columns 1,...,i-1. Hence no two tiles share a unit square.\" That's correct. They also claim \"By (1) the only uncovered squares are (i,i). The tile R_i contains only squares with column index > i, so it never contains (i,i). The tile L_i contains only squares with column index < i, so it never contains (i,i).\" That's fine.\n\nThus the only potential justification gaps are the lower bound claim's incomplete justification. But it's not a critical error; the claim is true and can be justified with a small addition. So classification: Justification Gap.\n\nNow we need to check if any construction claims are missing details. The construction is explicit: they define R_i and L_i with formulas. That's concrete details (Category C). So Level 2 passes.\n\nThus final verdict: PASS.\n\nNow we need to produce JSON with fields: verdict, confidence, issues, answer_correctness, reasoning.\n\nWe need to list issues: any justification gaps. So we should list the specific location: the lower bound claim about tile containing at most one element of S (and T). The issue: justification gap: missing explicit argument that tile containing two S squares must contain a diagonal square (i+1,i+1) in all cases, not only i+1=j.\n\nThus issues array should contain objects describing each issue. The schema is not explicitly defined beyond that it's an array. We can include objects with \"type\": \"Justification Gap\", \"location\": \"Claim about tile containing at most one element of S\", \"description\": \"Missing explicit justification that any rectangle containing two S squares must also contain a diagonal square, not only the case i+1=j.\"\n\nWe need to ensure JSON format is correct.\n\nNow confidence: we are very confident that answer is correct and reasoning valid. So maybe 0.99.\n\nNow answer_correctness: \"CORRECT\".\n\nNow reasoning: brief explanation: answer correct, reasoning uses valid methods, only minor justification gap.\n\nNow final JSON.\n\nThus output:\n\n{\n  \"verdict\": \"PASS\",\n  \"confidence\": 0.99,\n  \"issues\": [\n    {\n      \"type\": \"Justification Gap\",\n      \"location\": \"Lower bound argument: claim that each tile can contain at most one square of S (and similarly T)\",\n      \"description\": \"The proof only considers the case i+1=j and mentions (j,i+1) but does not explicitly show that for i+1<j the rectangle also contains a diagonal square (i+1,i+1), which would be uncovered. The missing detail does not affect validity but is a presentation gap.\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"answer_correctness\": \"CORRECT\",\n  \"reasoning\": \"The solution correctly reduces the problem to diagonal uncovered squares, proves a lower bound of 2n‑2 using a valid counting argument, and provides an explicit construction achieving that bound. All methods are valid; only a minor justification gap in the lower‑bound proof is present.\"\n}\n\nMake sure no extra text.",
