ng all of U; but we need at least n-1 tiles to cover S. So lower bound for covering U is at least n-1 tiles (maybe more). Similarly for L (lower triangle). So total lower bound is at least 2(n-1) = 2n-2. This is valid: any tiling must cover both S and T, each requiring at least n-1 distinct tiles. However, could a tile cover one element of S and one element of T simultaneously? The argument says each tile can contain at most one element of S, and at most one element of T. But a tile could contain one from S and one from T? Let's examine: Could a tile contain (i,i+1) and (j+1,j) for some i and j? Possibly if tile spans both above and below diagonal. But the tile would then also contain some diagonal squares? Let's examine: Suppose tile contains (i,i+1) (above diagonal) and (j+1,j) (below diagonal). The rectangle covering these two points would include the rectangle with rows from i to j+1 and columns from i+1 to j. But note that i+1 <= j? If i < j+1, then i+1 <= j? Not necessarily. Let's consider i = 1, j = 1: (1,2) and (2,1). The rectangle covering both includes (1,1) which is diagonal uncovered. So not allowed. In general, if tile contains a point above diagonal and a point below diagonal, the rectangle will include a diagonal point (the intersection of the row of the above point and column of the below point, or vice versa). So tile cannot contain both S and T elements. So each tile can contain at most one element from S ∪ T. So we need at least 2(n-1) tiles. So lower bound is valid.\n\nThus answer 2n-2 is correct.\n\nNow we need to check Level 2: Reasoning validity. The solution uses valid methods: combinatorial reasoning, case analysis, rectangle geometry, counting argument, construction. No invalid methods. The solution includes a construction with explicit details (tiles R_i and L_i). This is a concrete specification (Category C). So Level 2 passes.\n\nLevel 3: Presentation quality. Are there any critical errors? Let's examine the solution for any missing just