)\\)).  \n   - For each column \\(j=2,\\dots ,n\\) place a vertical tile covering \\((j+1,j),\\dots ,(n,j)\\) (size \\((n-j)\\times1\\)).  \n   These are \\( (n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2\\) disjoint rectangles that cover every square except the diagonal ones.\n\n5. **Intermediate numbers of tiles.**  \n   The maximal possible number of tiles is \\(n^{2}-n\\) (one \\(1\\times1\\) tile for each covered square).  \n   Starting from the minimal tiling (which contains many tiles of size \\(\\ge2\\)), we can repeatedly split a tile along a grid line; each split raises the tile count by exactly one and does not affect the uncovered squares. Consequently every integer  \n   \\[\n   t\\quad\\text{with}\\quad 2n-2\\le t\\le n^{2}-n\n   \\]\n   is achievable. For \\(n=2025\\) this yields all integers from \\(4048\\) up to \\(4\\,098\\,600\\).\n\n---\n\n**### Detailed Solution**\n\nLet \\(n=2025\\).  Index the rows from top to bottom by \\(i\\in[n]=\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) and the columns from left to right by \\(j\\in[n]\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  Uncovered squares form a permutation matrix  \n\nBecause each row and each column must contain exactly one uncovered unit square, there exists a permutation \\(\\sigma:[n]\\to[n]\\) such that the set of uncovered squares is  \n\n\\[\nU=\\{(i,\\sigma(i))\\mid i\\in[n]\\}.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  When can a rectangle be a tile?  \n\nFor non\u2011empty subsets \\(R\\subseteq[n]\\) (rows) and \\(C\\subseteq[n]\\) (columns) let  \n\n\\[\nR\\times C=\\{(i,j)\\mid i\\in R,\\;j\\in C\\}\n\\]\n\nbe the corresponding axis\u2011parallel rectangle.  \n\\(R\\times C\\) can be used as a tile **iff** it contains no uncovered square, i.e.\n\n\\[\n\\sigma(i)\\notin C\\qquad\\text{for every }i\\in R. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(\\sigma\\) is a bijection, \\(\\sigma(R)\\) has size \\(|R|\\).  Condition (1) says \\(\\sigma(R)\\subseteq[n]\\setminus C\\); therefore  \n\n\\[\n|R|\\le n-|C|\\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad |R|+|C|\\le n. \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nConversely, any pair \\((R,C)\\) satisfying (2) automatically satisfies (1) for the given \\(\\sigma\\).  Hence **every admissible tile must obey (2)**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Decomposition into a left and a right part  \n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\nL=\\{(i,j)\\mid j<\\sigma(i)\\},\\qquad  \nR'=\\{(i,j)\\mid j>\\sigma(i)\\}.\n\\]\n\nEvery covered square belongs to exactly one of these two sets.  \nA tile satisfying (2) is either **left** (all its squares lie in \\(L\\)) or **right** (all its squares lie in \\(R'\\)).  \nThus a tiling of the whole board consists of a collection of left tiles covering \\(L\\) and a collection of right tiles covering \\(R'\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Lower bound for the left part  \n\nFor \\(k\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) let  \n\n\\[\nL_k=\\{(i,k)\\mid \\sigma(i)>k\\}\n\\]\n\nbe the \\(k\\)-th column of \\(L\\).  \nBecause \\(\\sigma\\) is a bijection, exactly the rows whose hole lies to the right of column \\(k\\) belong to \\(L_k\\); there are \\(n-k\\) such rows, so  \n\n\\[\n|L_k|=n-k. \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nConsider a left tile \\(Q=R_Q\\times C_Q\\).  Let  \n\n\\[\nc(Q)=\\max C_Q\n\\]\n\nbe its rightmost column.  Since \\(Q\\subseteq L\\), we have \\(C_Q\\subseteq\\{1,\\dots ,c(Q)\\}\\) and by (2)  \n\n\\[\n|R_Q|\\le n-c(Q). \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nConsequently \\(Q\\) can contain at most \\(n-c(Q)\\) squares of any column \\(k\\le c(Q)\\).\n\nFix a column index \\(k\\).  All tiles covering \\(L_k\\) must satisfy \\(c(Q)\\ge k\\).  If a tile \\(Q\\) has \\(c(Q)>k\\), then by (4) it contributes at most \\(n-c(Q)\\le n-(k+1)\\) squares to \\(L_k\\).  Since \\(|L_k|=n-k\\) by (3), at least one tile covering \\(L_k\\) must have \\(c(Q)=k\\).\n\nThus for each \\(k=1,\\dots ,n-1\\) there exists a tile whose rightmost column equals \\(k\\).  Different values of \\(k\\) give different tiles (a tile has a unique rightmost column).  Hence any partition of \\(L\\) uses **at least \\(n-1\\) left tiles**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  Lower bound for the right part  \n\nThe set \\(R'\\) is the transpose of \\(L\\) after swapping rows and columns (replace each square \\((i,j)\\) by \\((j,i)\\)).  Applying the argument of \u00a74 to the transposed board yields that at least \\(n-1\\) right tiles are necessary.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 6.  Global lower bound  \n\nCombining the two halves,\n\n\\[\n\\#\\text{tiles}\\ge (n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2. \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 7.  Construction attaining the bound  \n\nChoose the permutation \\(\\sigma(i)=i\\) (holes on the main diagonal).  \n\n*Horizontal tiles.*  \nFor each row \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) place the rectangle  \n\n\\[\nH_i=\\{(i,j)\\mid j=i+1,\\dots ,n\\},\n\\]\n\na \\(1\\times (n-i)\\) tile.  All squares of \\(H_i\\) satisfy \\(j>\\sigma(i)=i\\); hence the \\(H_i\\)\u2019s lie in the right part \\(R'\\).\n\n*Vertical tiles.*  \nFor each column \\(j\\in\\{2,\\dots ,n\\}\\) place the rectangle  \n\n\\[\nV_j=\\{(i,j)\\mid i=j+1,\\dots ,n\\},\n\\]\n\nan \\((n-j)\\times1\\) tile.  Every square of \\(V_j\\) satisfies \\(j<\\sigma(i)\\); thus the \\(V_j\\)\u2019s lie in the left part \\(L\\).\n\nThe families \\(\\{H_i\\}\\) and \\(\\{V_j\\}\\) are disjoint (one lies strictly above the diagonal, the other strictly below).  Together they cover every square of the board except the diagonal squares \\((i,i)\\), which are precisely the uncovered squares required.  The number of tiles is  \n\n\\[\n|\\,\\{H_i\\}\\,|+|\\,\\{V_j\\}\\,|=(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2.\n\\]\n\nHence the lower bound (5) is tight.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 8.  Minimum for \\(n=2025\\)\n\nSubstituting \\(n=2025\\) gives  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{2\\cdot2025-2=4048}.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 9.  All intermediate numbers of tiles are achievable  \n\nThe maximal possible number of tiles occurs when each covered square is a separate \\(1\\times1\\) tile; there are \\(n^{2}-n\\) such squares, so the maximum is \\(n^{2}-n\\).\n\nStarting from the minimal tiling described in \u00a77, note that at least \\(2n-4\\) of its tiles have area \\(\\ge2\\).  Choose any tile of size \\(a\\times b\\) with \\(ab\\ge2\\) and cut it along a grid line into two smaller rectangles.  This operation increases the total number of tiles by exactly one and does not affect the set of uncovered squares.  Repeating the operation we can increase the tile count step\u2011by\u2011step until every covered square becomes a \\(1\\times1\\) tile.\n\nTherefore **every integer**  \n\n\\[\nt\\quad\\text{with}\\quad 2n-2\\le t\\le n^{2}-n\n\\]\n\ncan be realized.  For \\(n=2025\\) this means that every integer  \n\n\\[\n4048\\le t\\le4\\,098\\,600\n\\]\n\nis achievable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Conclusion.**  \nThe smallest possible number of rectangular tiles is \\(4048\\).  Moreover, any integer between \\(4048\\) and the trivial upper bound \\(4\\,098\\,600\\) can be obtained by an appropriate tiling."
