            "content": "final answer.\n\n**### Summary ###  \n\n**a. Verdict:**  \nI have successfully solved the problem. The minimum number of rectangular tiles required is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{4048}.\n\\]\n\n**b. Method Sketch:**  \n1. **Model the uncovered squares.**  \n   – Let \\(n=2025\\).  \n   – In each row \\(i\\) the uncovered unit square is at column \\(p(i)\\), where \\(p\\) is a permutation of \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).  \n   – The squares left of the hole in row \\(i\\) form the interval \\([1,p(i)-1]\\); the squares right of the hole form \\([p(i)+1,n]\\).  \n\n2. **Separate the two sides.**  \n   – No tile can contain a hole, therefore every tile lies entirely on the left side or entirely on the right side of the holes.  \n   – The total number of tiles equals the number of tiles covering the left side plus the number covering the right side.\n\n3. **Lower bound for each side.**  \n   – For the left side define \\(a_i=p(i)-1\\). Because \\(p\\) is a permutation, \\(\\{a_i\\}=\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\).  \n   – Any rectangle covering a set of rows \\(I\\) must be of the form \\(I\\times[1,t]\\) with \\(t\\le\\min_{i\\in I}a_i\\).  \n   – The smallest positive \\(a_i\\) in a rectangle is forced to be covered by that rectangle, and distinct positive \\(a_i\\)’s cannot share the same rectangle.  \n   – Hence at least \\(n-1\\) rectangles are needed to cover the left side.  \n   – The same argument applied to the right side (using \\(b_i=n-p(i)\\)) gives another lower bound of \\(n-1\\).  \n   – Consequently any tiling uses at least \\(2(n-1)\\) tiles.\n\n4. **Construction achieving the bound.**  \n   – Order the rows so that \\(a_{r_1}=0<a_{r_2}=1<\\dots<a_{r_n}=n-1\\).  \n   – For each \\(k=2,\\dots ,n\\) place the rectangle  \n\n\\[\nL_k=\\{r_k,r_{k+1},\\dots ,r_n\\}\\times\\{a_{r_k}\\},\n\\]\n\n   which is a vertical strip of width 1 covering column \\(a_{r_k}\\) in all rows whose left interval is at least that long.  \n   – These \\(n-1\\) rectangles are pairwise disjoint and together cover every square on the left side.  \n\n   – Apply the same construction to the reflected board (swap column \\(j\\) with column \\(n+1-j\\)). This yields \\(n-1\\) disjoint rectangles covering the right side.  \n\n   – Altogether we obtain exactly \\(2(n-1)\\) tiles, meeting the lower bound.\n\n5. **Conclusion.**  \n   – The lower and upper bounds coincide, so the minimum possible number of tiles is \\(2n-2\\).  \n   – For \\(n=2025\\) this equals \\(2\\cdot2025-2=4048\\).\n\n**### Detailed Solution ###**  \n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nn=2025,\n\\qquad\np:\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\to\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\n\\]\n\nbe a permutation.  \nIn row \\(i\\) the unique uncovered unit square is \\((i,p(i))\\).  \nDefine  \n\n\\[\na_i=p(i)-1\\qquad (0\\le a_i\\le n-1),\\qquad \nb_i=n-p(i)\\qquad (0\\le b_i\\le n-1).\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(p\\) is a permutation,  \n\n\\[\n\\{a_1,\\dots ,a_n\\}=\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\n\\quad\\text{and}\\quad\n\\{b_1,\\dots ,b_n\\}=\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\}.\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nThe set of covered squares splits into two disjoint parts  \n\n\\[\nL=\\{(i,j):1\\le i\\le n,\\;1\\le j\\le a_i\\},\n\\qquad\nR=\\{(i,j):1\\le i\\le n,\\;p(i)+1\\le j\\le n\\}.\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nA rectangular tile cannot contain a hole, therefore each tile lies completely in \\(L\\) or completely in \\(R\\).  \nConsequently  \n\n\\[\n\\#\\text{tiles}= \\#\\text{tiles covering }L+\\#\\text{tiles covering }R .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1 (lower bound for \\(L\\))\n\nAny tiling of \\(L\\) by axis‑aligned rectangles uses at least \\(n-1\\) rectangles.\n\n*Proof.*  \nConsider a rectangle \\(Q\\) that covers a non‑empty set of rows \\(I\\subseteq\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).  \nSince every row of \\(L\\) is left‑aligned, the column interval of \\(Q\\) must be of the form \\([1,t]\\) for some integer \\(t\\).  \nBecause \\(Q\\) must contain the rightmost square of each row it covers, we must have  \n\n\\[\nt=\\min_{i\\in I} a_i .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nLet \\(i_0\\in I\\) be a row attaining this minimum; then \\(a_{i_0}=t\\).  \nThe square \\((i_0,t)\\) belongs to \\(L\\) and is covered by \\(Q\\); no other rectangle can contain this square, for any other rectangle covering row \\(i_0\\) would have a column interval \\([1,t']\\) with \\(t'\\le a_{i_0}=t\\), and the equality \\(t'=t\\) would force the two rectangles to coincide on that column, contradicting the requirement that tiles are disjoint.  \n\nThus each rectangle is uniquely associated with the row whose \\(a_i\\) is minimal among the rows it covers.  \nAll rows with \\(a_i>0\\) must appear as such a minimal row, because otherwise the square \\((i,a_i)\\) would remain uncovered.  \nSince by (1) there are exactly \\(n-1\\) rows with \\(a_i>0\\), at least \\(n-1\\) rectangles are necessary. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2 (lower bound for \\(R\\))\n\nAnalogously, any tiling of \\(R\\) needs at least \\(n-1\\) rectangles.\n\n*Proof.*  \nReplace each column \\(j\\) by the reflected column \\(n+1-j\\).  \nUnder this reflection the set \\(R\\) becomes a left‑aligned region of the same type, with the numbers \\(b_i\\) playing the role of the \\(a_i\\).  \nApplying Lemma 1 to the reflected picture yields the claim. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\nFrom Lemmas 1 and 2 and (3) we obtain the universal lower bound  \n\n\\[\n\\#\\text{tiles}\\ge (n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2 .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Construction attaining \\(2n-2\\) tiles  \n\nWe now exhibit a tiling with exactly \\(2n-2\\) rectangles.\n\n#### Covering the left side \\(L\\)\n\nOrder the rows so that  \n\n\\[\na_{r_1}=0<a_{r_2}=1<\\dots <a_{r_n}=n-1 .\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nFor each \\(k=2,\\dots ,n\\) define  \n\n\\[\nL_k=\\{\\,r_k,r_{k+1},\\dots ,r_n\\,\\}\\times\\{\\,a_{r_k}\\,\\}.\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\n*Why \\(L_k\\subseteq L\\).*  \nIf \\(i\\ge k\\) then \\(a_i\\ge a_{r_k}\\) by (6); hence the square \\((i,a_{r_k})\\) belongs to \\(L\\).  \n\n*Disjointness.*  \nThe column used in \\(L_k\\) is \\(a_{r_k}=k-1\\), which is different for different values of \\(k\\); therefore the rectangles \\(L_2,\\dots ,L_n\\) are pairwise disjoint.  \n\n*Coverage.*  \nTake any square \\((i,j)\\in L\\). Then \\(j\\le a_i\\). Let \\(k=j+1\\); by (6) we have \\(a_{r_k}=j\\). Moreover \\(a_i\\ge j\\) implies \\(i\\in\\{r_k,\\dots ,r_n\\}\\). Hence \\((i,j)\\in L_k\\). Thus the family \\(\\{L_k\\}_{k=2}^n\\) tiles \\(L\\) with exactly \\(n-1\\) rectangles.\n\n#### Covering the right side \\(R\\)\n\nReflect the board horizontally: replace column \\(j\\) by \\(j' = n+1-j\\).  \nUnder this transformation the right side \\(R\\) becomes a left‑aligned region whose left‑interval lengths are precisely \\(b_i\\).  \nApplying the previous construction to the reflected board yields rectangles  \n\n\\[\nR_k'=\\{\\,s_k,s_{k+1},\\dots ,s_n\\,\\}\\times\\{\\,b_{s_k}\\,\\},\n\\qquad k=2,\\dots ,n,\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nwhere the rows are ordered so that  \n\n\\[\nb_{s_1}=0<b_{s_2}=1<\\dots <b_{s_n}=n-1 .\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nReflecting these rectangles back to the original board gives \\(n-1\\) disjoint rectangles covering \\(R\\).\n\n#### Total number of tiles\n\nThe left side contributes \\(n-1\\) rectangles, the right side another \\(n-1\\); together we obtain a tiling of the whole board with  \n\n\\[\n2(n-1)=2n-2\n\\]\n\ntiles.  \n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Optimality\n\nThe lower bound (5) and the explicit construction both give the same number \\(2n-2\\).  \nHence this number is the minimum possible.\n\nFor the concrete size \\(n=2025\\),\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{2\\cdot2025-2=4048}\n\\]\n\ntiles are necessary and sufficient. ∎"
