\) (using the numbers \\(b_i\\)) yields at least \\(n-1\\) rectangles for \\(R\\).\n\n   Consequently  \n\n   \\[\n   \\#\\text{tiles}\\ge (n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2 .\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n4. **Construction attaining the bound.**  \n\n   *Covering \\(L\\).*  \n   Order the rows so that  \n\n   \\[\n   a_{r_1}=0<a_{r_2}=1<\\dots<a_{r_n}=n-1 .\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   For each \\(k=2,\\dots ,n\\) define the rectangle  \n\n   \\[\n   L_k=\\{\\,r_k,r_{k+1},\\dots ,r_n\\,\\}\\times\\{\\,a_{r_k}\\,\\}.\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   Because the rows are sorted, every row \\(r_i\\) with \\(i\\ge k\\) satisfies \\(a_{r_i}\\ge a_{r_k}\\); thus the squares \\((r_i,a_{r_k})\\) belong to \\(L\\), and (4) is indeed a rectangle (a vertical strip of width\u202f1).  \n   The columns used in the rectangles are distinct (\\(a_{r_k}=k-1\\)), so the rectangles \\(L_2,\\dots ,L_n\\) are pairwise disjoint.  \n\n   To see that they cover all of \\(L\\), take any \\((i,j)\\in L\\). Then \\(j\\le a_i\\). Let \\(k=j+1\\). By (3) we have \\(a_{r_k}=j\\), and since \\(a_i\\ge j\\), the index of row \\(i\\) in the ordered list is at least \\(k\\); hence \\(i\\in\\{r_k,\\dots ,r_n\\}\\). Therefore \\((i,j)\\in L_k\\). Thus \\(\\{L_k\\}_{k=2}^n\\) tiles \\(L\\) with exactly \\(n-1\\) rectangles.\n\n   *Covering \\(R\\).*  \n   Reflect the board horizontally (replace column \\(j\\) by \\(j'=n+1-j\\)). Under this reflection the region \\(R\\) becomes a left\u2011aligned region whose row lengths are precisely the numbers \\(b_i\\). Applying the construction above to the reflected board yields \\(n-1\\) vertical strips covering the reflected region. Reflecting these strips back gives \\(n-1\\) disjoint rectangles covering the original region \\(R\\).\n\n   *Total number of tiles.*  \n   The left side contributes \\(n-1\\) rectangles, the right side another \\(n-1\\); together we obtain a tiling of the whole board (except the holes) with  \n\n   \\[\n   2(n-1)=2n-2\n   \\]\n\n   tiles.\n\n5. **Conclusion.**  \n   The lower bound (2) and the explicit construction both give the same number \\(2n-2\\). Hence this is the minimal possible number of tiles. For \\(n=2025\\),\n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{2\\cdot2025-2=4048}.\n   \\]\n\n   No tiling can use fewer tiles, and a tiling with exactly \\(4048\\) tiles exists. \u220e\n\n--- \n\n**Detailed Solution**  \n\nLet \\(n=2025\\).  \nBecause each row and each column must contain exactly one uncovered unit square, the set of uncovered squares is a permutation matrix: there exists a permutation \\(p:\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\to\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) such that the unique hole in row \\(i\\) is the unit square \\((i,p(i))\\).\n\nDefine for each row \\(i\\)\n\n\\[\na_i=p(i)-1,\\qquad b_i=n-p(i).\n\\]\n\nThus \\(0\\le a_i,b_i\\le n-1\\) and, since \\(p\\) is a permutation,\n\n\\[\n\\{a_1,\\dots ,a_n\\}=\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\},\\qquad \n\\{b_1,\\dots ,b_n\\}=\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\}. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nThe covered squares split into two disjoint regions\n\n\\[\nL=\\{(i,j):1\\le i\\le n,\\;1\\le j\\le a_i\\},\n\\qquad\nR=\\{(i,j):1\\le i\\le n,\\;p(i)+1\\le j\\le n\\}.\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nA rectangular tile cannot contain a hole; therefore each tile lies entirely in \\(L\\) or entirely in \\(R\\). Consequently\n\n\\[\n\\#\\text{tiles}= \\#\\text{tiles covering }L+\\#\\text{tiles covering }R. \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1 (lower bound for \\(L\\))\n\nAny tiling of \\(L\\) by axis\u2011aligned rectangles uses at least \\(n-1\\) rectangles.\n\n*Proof.*  \nConsider a rectangle \\(Q\\) that covers a non\u2011empty interval of rows \\([r_1,r_2]\\). Because \\(L\\) is left\u2011aligned, the column interval of \\(Q\\) must be \\([1,t]\\) for some integer \\(t\\). The rectangle must be contained in \\(L\\), so we must have  \n\n\\[\nt\\le a_i\\quad\\text{for every }i\\in[r_1,r_2].\n\\]\n\nHence  \n\n\\[\nt=\\min_{i\\in[r_1,r_2]}a_i. \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nLet \\(i_0\\in[r_1,r_2]\\) be a row where the minimum in (4) is attained; then \\(a_{i_0}=t\\). The square \\((i_0,t)\\) belongs to \\(L\\) and is covered by \\(Q\\). No other rectangle can contain this square, because any rectangle covering row \\(i_0\\) must have column interval \\([1,t']\\) with \\(t'\\le a_{i_0}=t\\); if \\(t'=t\\) the rectangle would coincide with \\(Q\\) on that column, and if \\(t'<t\\) the square \\((i_0,t)\\) would be uncovered. Since tiles are required to be disjoint, \\((i_0,t)\\) is unique to \\(Q\\).\n\nNow consider any row \\(i\\) with \\(a_i>0\\). Its rightmost covered square is \\((i,a_i)\\). This square must belong to some rectangle; by the previous paragraph the rectangle covering it must have width \\(t=a_i\\) and must contain a row where the minimum of the \\(a\\)-values equals \\(a_i\\). Because the values \\(a_i\\) are distinct for \\(a_i>0\\) (by (1)), row \\(i\\) is the **only** row attaining that minimum. Hence each row with \\(a_i>0\\) forces a distinct rectangle. There are exactly \\(n-1\\) such rows (the unique row with \\(a_i=0\\) does not contribute). Therefore any tiling of \\(L\\) needs at least \\(n-1\\) rectangles. \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2 (lower bound for \\(R\\))\n\nAnalogously, any tiling of \\(R\\) requires at least \\(n-1\\) rectangles.\n\n*Proof.*  \nApply a horizontal reflection: map column \\(j\\) to \\(j'=n+1-j\\). Under this map the region \\(R\\) becomes a left\u2011aligned region whose row lengths are the numbers \\(b_i\\). Since the multiset \\(\\{b_i\\}\\) also equals \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) by (1), Lemma\u202f1 applied to the reflected picture yields the claim. \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\nFrom Lemmas\u202f1 and\u202f2 and (3) we obtain the universal lower bound\n\n\\[\n\\#\\text{tiles}\\ge (n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2. \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Construction attaining \\(2n-2\\) tiles  \n\nWe now exhibit a tiling with exactly \\(2n-2\\) rectangles.\n\n#### Covering the left side \\(L\\)\n\nReorder the rows so that their \\(a\\)-values are increasing:\n\n\\[\na_{r_1}=0<a_{r_2}=1<\\dots<a_{r_n}=n-1. \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nFor each \\(k=2,\\dots ,n\\) define the rectangle  \n\n\\[\nL_k=\\{\\,r_k,r_{k+1},\\dots ,r_n\\,\\}\\times\\{\\,a_{r_k}\\,\\}. \\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nBecause of (6), for any row \\(r_i\\) with \\(i\\ge k\\) we have \\(a_{r_i}\\ge a_{r_k}\\); consequently the squares \\((r_i,a_{r_k})\\) belong to \\(L\\). Thus \\(L_k\\) is a legitimate rectangle (a vertical strip of width\u202f1). The columns used are distinct (\\(a_{r_k}=k-1\\)), so the rectangles \\(L_2,\\dots ,L_n\\) are pairwise disjoint.\n\nTo see that they cover all of \\(L\\), let \\((i,j)\\in L\\). Then \\(j\\le a_i\\). Set \\(k=j+1\\). By (6) we have \\(a_{r_k}=j\\). Since \\(a_i\\ge j\\), the index of row \\(i\\) in the ordered list is at least \\(k\\); therefore \\(i\\in\\{r_k,\\dots ,r_n\\}\\). Hence \\((i,j)\\in L_k\\). Thus \\(\\{L_k\\}_{k=2}^n\\) tiles \\(L\\) with exactly \\(n-1\\) rectangles.\n\n#### Covering the right side \\(R\\)\n\nReflect the board horizontally (replace column \\(j\\) by \\(n+1-j\\)). The reflected right side becomes a left\u2011aligned region with row lengths \\(b_i\\). Applying the construction above to the reflected board yields rectangles  \n\n\\[\nR_k'=\\{\\,s_k,s_{k+1},\\dots ,s_n\\,\\}\\times\\{\\,b_{s_k}\\,\\},\n\\qquad k=2,\\dots ,n,\n\\]\n\nwhere the rows are ordered so that  \n\n\\[\nb_{s_1}=0<b_{s_2}=1<\\dots<b_{s_n}=n-1.\n\\]\n\nReflecting these rectangles back to the original board gives \\(n-1\\) disjoint rectangles covering \\(R\\). (Each reflected rectangle occupies a distinct column of the original board, namely columns \\(n,n-1,\\dots ,2\\).)\n\n#### Total number of tiles\n\nThe left side contributes \\(n-1\\) rectangles, the right side another \\(n-1\\); together we obtain a tiling of the whole board (except the holes) with  \n\n\\[\n2(n-1)=2n-2\n\\]\n\ntiles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Optimality\n\nThe lower bound (5) and the explicit construction both give the same number \\(2n-2\\). Hence this number is the minimum possible.\n\nFor the given grid size \\(n=2025\\),\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{2\\cdot2025-2=4048}.\n\\]\n\nThus Matilda can achieve the required condition with \\(4048\\) tiles, and she cannot do it with fewer. \u220e"
