onsequently \\(L\\) contains precisely  \n\n\\[\nn-1\n\\]\n\nleft‑corners.\n\n*Proof that a rectangle in \\(L\\) contains at most one left‑corner.*  \nLet \\(R=[a,b]\\times[c,d]\\) be a rectangle contained in \\(L\\).  Suppose it contains two distinct left‑corners \\((i_1,p(i_1)-1)\\) and \\((i_2,p(i_2)-1)\\) with \\(i_1<i_2\\).  Since the column interval \\([c,d]\\) is contiguous and contains both columns \\(p(i_1)-1\\) and \\(p(i_2)-1\\), it also contains the larger column \\(p(i_2)-1\\).  Then the cell \\((i_1,p(i_2)-1)\\) belongs to the rectangle.  Because \\(p(i_2)-1\\ge p(i_1)\\), this cell lies **to the right** of the hole \\((i_1,p(i_1))\\) and therefore is **not** in \\(L\\) – a contradiction.  Hence a rectangle of \\(L\\) can contain at most one left‑corner. ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### Lemma 2 (corners of \\(R\\))\n\nFor every row \\(i\\) with \\(p(i)<n\\) the cell  \n\n\\[\nc_i^{R}=(i,p(i)+1)\n\\]\n\nbelongs to \\(R\\).  These are the **right‑corners**.  Exactly one row has \\(p(i)=n\\); consequently \\(R\\) contains exactly  \n\n\\[\nn-1\n\\]\n\nright‑corners.\n\n*Proof that a rectangle in \\(R\\) contains at most one right‑corner* is completely symmetric to Lemma 1.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### Lemma 3 (no rectangle meets both \\(L\\) and \\(R\\))\n\nAssume a rectangle \\(T\\) contains a cell of \\(L\\) and a cell of \\(R\\).  \nThen there exist rows \\(i\\) and columns \\(j_1<j_2\\) such that \\((i,j_1)\\in L\\) and \\((i,j_2)\\in R\\).  \nSince the column interval of \\(T\\) is contiguous, it contains the whole interval \\([j_1,j_2]\\) and therefore also the cell \\((i,p(i))\\) (the unique hole in row \\(i\\)).  This contradicts the requirement that no tile may cover a hole.  Hence every tile lies entirely in \\(L\\) or entirely in \\(R\\). ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### Lemma 4 (lower bound)\n\nLet \