ximum antichain equals the length of the longest decreasing subsequence of the row lengths? Not exactly.\n\nBut we can think about covering L with rectangles. Since L is left-justified, each rectangle covering L must be of the form rows I and columns J where J is a prefix of columns up to some column c, and I is a set of rows where p(i)-1 >= c (i.e., rows that have at least c columns). So each rectangle in L can be thought of as covering a \"block\" of rows and columns up to some column. This is reminiscent of covering a Young diagram with \"hooks\" or \"horizontal strips\". The minimal number of rectangles needed to cover a Young diagram is equal to the number of rows of the diagram? Actually we can cover each row's left region with a single rectangle per row (horizontal tile of height 1). That's n-1 tiles (since one row has no left region). That's the solution's vertical tiles for L (vertical tiles covering columns). Wait, they used vertical tiles for L: each column j (except the last) has a vertical tile covering rows i where p(i) > j+1? Actually they used vertical tiles V_i = rows i..n, column i-1. That covers left region column by column. That's also n-1 tiles. So they used a column-based covering.\n\nBut could we cover L with fewer than n-1 rectangles? Possibly if some rows have left region that can be combined into a rectangle spanning multiple rows and columns. However, due to holes, any rectangle spanning multiple rows must have its column range be a prefix that is less than the smallest p(i) among those rows (to avoid holes). So the rectangle can only extend up to min_i p(i)-1 columns. So if we have rows with different p(i) values, the rectangle can only go up to the smallest p(i)-1 among them. This may limit the ability to combine rows.\n\nSimilarly for R.\n\nThus the lower bound of n-1 for each side may be tight, but we need to prove it. The solution attempted to prove it using left-corners and right-corners, but the proof is flawed because they assumed 