y proven, but maybe it's still true? Let's examine more carefully.\n\nDefine L = set of cells (i,j) with j < p(i). R = set of cells (i,j) with j > p(i). For a rectangle T = I x J (I interval of rows, J interval of columns). Suppose T contains a cell (i1,j1) ∈ L and a cell (i2,j2) ∈ R. Then j1 < p(i1) and j2 > p(i2). Since J is interval, it contains all columns between j1 and j2. So for any column c between j1 and j2, c ∈ J. In particular, consider the column p(i1). If p(i1) is between j1 and j2, then (i1,p(i1)) ∈ T, which is uncovered square, contradiction. Similarly for p(i2). So to avoid covering uncovered squares, we need p(i1) ≤ j1 or p(i1) ≥ j2. But we have j1 < p(i1) (since (i1,j1) ∈ L). So p(i1) > j1. So we need p(i1) ≥ j2. So p(i1) ≥ j2 > p(i2). So p(i1) > p(i2). So p(i1) > p(i2). So we need p(i1) ≥ j2 > p(i2). So p(i1) > p(i2). That is possible. So p(i1) > p(i2). So p(i1) is larger than p(i2). Then p(i1) might be > j2, so p(i1) is not in J. Similarly p(i2) < j1? Wait, we have j2 > p(i2). So p(i2) < j2. But p(i2) could be less than j1? Not necessarily. But we need to avoid p(i2) being in J. Since J contains all columns from j1 to j2, if p(i2) is between j1 and j2, then (i2,p(i2)) ∈ T, contradiction. So we need p(i2) ≤ j1. Since j1 < p(i1), we have p(i2) ≤ j1 < p(i1). So p(i2) < p(i1). So p(i2) < j1. So we need p(i2) ≤ j1. So we need j1 ≥ p(i2). But we also have j1 < p(i1). So we need p(i2) ≤ j1 < p(i1). So we need p(i2) ≤ j1 < p(i1). So we need a column j1 that lies between p(i2) and p(i1) (including possibly equal to p(i2)). But j1 must be less than p(i1) and greater or equal to p(i2). So j1 can be p(i2) or any column between p(i2) and p(i1)-1. So we can choose j1 = p(i2). But j1 must be < p(i1) and also j1 < p(i1) holds. However, j1 must be a column such that (i1,j1) ∈ L, i.e., j1 < p(i1). If j1 = p(i2) and p(i2) < p(i1), then j1 < p(i1) holds. So (i1,p(i2)) ∈ L. Similarly, we need j2 > p(i2) and j2 ≤ p(i1) maybe? Actually we need j2 > p(i2) and j2 ≤ p(i1)