ich are two monotone shapes: L is a shape formed by left of each hole, which is a \"lower-left\" shape that is not necessarily monotone? Let's examine shape L: For each row i, columns j < p(i) are included. This shape is a \"Ferrers board\" if the permutation is 132-avoiding? Not necessarily. Similarly R is shape of cells to the right.\n\nWe need to tile L ∪ R with rectangles that avoid holes. This is essentially a covering problem. The minimal number of rectangles needed to cover a Ferrers board (a Young diagram) is known to be equal to the number of rows (or columns) maybe? Actually covering a Ferrers board with rectangles (monotone shape) is related to the \"rectangle covering number\" which equals the size of a maximum antichain in the poset of cells? Not sure.\n\nBut the problem is more specific: we have two disjoint shapes L and R. Each shape is a \"skew shape\"? Actually L is a \"southwest\" shape: for each row i, columns 1..p(i)-1 are included. This shape is a \"left-justified\" shape: each row's left region is a prefix of columns. So L is a left-justified shape (Ferrers diagram) where row lengths are p(i)-1. The row lengths may not be monotone (they can go up and down). However, left-justified shape can be covered by rectangles in a certain way. The minimal number of rectangles needed to cover a left-justified shape is equal to the number of distinct row lengths? Actually there is known result: The rectangle covering number of a Young diagram equals the size of the largest antichain in the poset of cells (by Dilworth's theorem). For a Ferrers board, the largest antichain is the maximum number of cells no two of which are comparable in the partial order (i.e., not in same row or column). This is the size of a maximum matching? Actually the poset is defined by (i,j) ≤ (i',j') if i ≤ i' and j ≤ j'. An antichain is a set of cells where no one is ≤ another, i.e., they are in different rows and columns and not comparable. For a Ferrers shape, the size of a maximum antichain equals the length of the longest decreasing subsequence of the row lengths? Not exactly.\n\nBut we can think about covering L with rectangles. Since L is left-justified, each rectangle covering L must be of the form rows I and columns J where J is a prefix of columns up to some column c, and I is a set of rows where p(i)-1 >= c (i.e., rows that have at least c columns). So each rectangle in L can be thought of as covering a \"block\" of rows and columns up to some column. This is reminiscent of covering a Young diagram with \"hooks\" or \"horizontal strips\". The minimal number of rectangles needed to cover a Young diagram is equal to the number of rows of the diagram? Actually we can cover each row's left region with a single rectangle per row (horizontal tile of height 1). That's n-1 tiles (since one row has no left region). That's the solution's vertical tiles for L (vertical tiles covering columns). Wait, they used vertical tiles for L: each column j (except the last) has a vertical tile covering rows i where p(i) > j+1? Actually they used vertical tiles V_i = rows i..n, column i-1. That covers left region column by column. That's also n-1 tiles. So they used a column-based covering.\n\nBut could we cover L with fewer than n-1 rectangles? Possibly if some rows have left region that can be combined into a rectangle spanning multiple rows and columns. However, due to holes, any rectangle spanning multiple rows must have its column range be a prefix that is less than the smallest p(i) among those rows (to avoid holes). So the rectangle can only extend up to min_i p(i)-1 columns. So if we have rows with different p(i) values, the rectangle can only go up to the smallest p(i)-1 among them. This may limit the ability to combine rows.\n\nSimilarly for R.\n\nThus the lower bound of n-1 for each side may be tight, but we need to prove it. The solution attempted to prove it using left-corners and right-corners, but the proof is flawed because they assumed each