                    "reasoning_content": "We need to read the problem and solution, then apply hierarchical decision tree with the extra constraints. The solution is fairly detailed: they define permutation matrix, left/right corners, lemmas, lower bound, construction, answer 4048.\n\nWe need to verify if answer is correct, reasoning valid, and any presentation issues.\n\nWe must output JSON with fields: verdict (PASS/FAIL), confidence (0-1), issues (list), answer_correctness (CORRECT/INCORRECT/INCOMPLETE/UNKNOWN), reasoning (brief explanation). Also we need to follow hierarchical decision tree: Level 1 answer correctness, Level 2 reasoning validity, Level 3 presentation issues (justification gaps vs critical errors). The final verdict is PASS if answer correct and reasoning valid, regardless of justification gaps (unless there are critical errors). So we need to check.\n\nFirst, answer: 4048. Is that correct? Let's think about the problem: 2025x2025 grid, each row and column must have exactly one uncovered unit square. So we need to cover all other squares with rectangles (tiles) that don't overlap and each tile must be a rectangle aligned to grid lines. We need to minimize number of tiles. The solution claims minimum is 2n-2 = 2*2025 - 2 = 4048. Let's verify if that is plausible.\n\nWe need to check if any lower bound could be lower. The solution uses left and right corners argument to get lower bound 2n-2. Let's see if we can find a tiling with fewer tiles? Let's think about the problem more generally: For an n x n board with exactly one uncovered square per row and column, we have a permutation matrix of holes. The uncovered squares are at positions (i,p(i)). The rest of the board is covered by rectangles, no overlaps. The solution shows that any rectangle cannot cross the permutation line (the holes) because then it would contain a hole. So each rectangle lies entirely left of the hole in its row (i.e., columns less than p(i)) or entirely right of the hole (columns greater than p(i)). So the board is partitioned into left region L and right region R. Then they argue each rectangle in L can contain at most one left-corner (i,p(i)-1) and each rectangle in R can contain at most one right-corner (i,p(i)+1). Since there are n-1 left corners and n-1 right corners, we need at least 2n-2 rectangles. That seems plausible.\n\nBut is the lemma that each rectangle in L can contain at most one left-corner correct? Let's verify: L is defined as cells left of the hole in each row. So for each row i, the left region includes columns j < p(i). The left-corner is the cell (i,p(i)-1) if p(i) > 1. So each row except the one where p(i)=1 has a left-corner. So there are n-1 left-corners. The lemma says any rectangle in L can contain at most one left-corner. The proof: Suppose rectangle T = I x J is in L. For each i in I, all columns in J must be < p(i). So the maximum column index d = max J must satisfy d < p(i) for all i in I, i.e., d < min_i p(i). So d < min_{i in I} p(i). If rectangle contains a left-corner (i,p(i)-1), then p(i)-1 ∈ J, so p(i)-1 ≤ d, thus p(i) ≤ d+1. Combine with d+1 ≤ min_i p(i) => p(i) ≤ min_i p(i). So p(i) = min_i p(i). So all rows i whose left-corner is in T have p(i) equal to the minimum p among rows in I. Since p is a permutation, the same p value cannot appear for two different rows. So there can be at most one such row, i.e., |I| = 1. So rectangle occupies a single row. So it can contain at most one left-corner. That seems correct.\n\nSimilarly for right-corner lemma.\n\nThus lower bound is correct.\n\nConstruction: They choose p(i) = i (main diagonal). Then left region L is strictly left of diagonal, right region R is strictly right of diagonal. They define horizontal tiles H_i = {i} x {i+1,...,n} for i=1,...,n-1 (covering the part of row i to the right of diagonal). That's a 1 x (n-i) tile. And vertical tiles V_i = {i,...,n} x {i-1} for i=2,...,n (covering the part of column i-1 below diagonal). That's (n-i+1) x 1