 ≤ p(i1) - maybe j2 could be p(i1)? But j2 must be > p(i2) and ≤ p(i1). If j2 = p(i1), then (i2,p(i1)) ∈ R? Wait, for (i2,j2) ∈ R we need j2 > p(i2). So if j2 = p(i1) > p(i2), then (i2,p(i1)) ∈ R. So we can have rectangle covering columns from p(i2) to p(i1). Then J = [p(i2), p(i1)], I includes rows i1 and i2. Then rectangle contains (i1,p(i2)) ∈ L (since p(i2) < p(i1)), and (i2,p(i1)) ∈ R (since p(i1) > p(i2)). Does rectangle contain any uncovered squares? It contains (i1,p(i1))? No, p(i1) is the right endpoint of J, so column p(i1) is in J. So (i1,p(i1)) ∈ T, which is uncovered square. So rectangle includes uncovered square. So not allowed. So we need to avoid including p(i1) as well. So we need j2 < p(i1). So J = [p(i2), p(i1)-1] perhaps. Then J includes columns from p(i2) to p(i1)-1. Then (i1,p(i1)) is not in J, (i2,p(i2)) is not in J (since p(i2) is left endpoint, but p(i2) is in J? Actually J includes p(i2) as left endpoint, so (i2,p(i2)) is uncovered square for row i2. So rectangle includes uncovered square. So not allowed.\n\nThus any rectangle that contains a left cell for some row i1 and a right cell for some row i2 must contain either (i1,p(i1)) or (i2,p(i2)). Let's try to prove this formally:\n\nAssume T = I x J, with I interval of rows, J interval of columns. Suppose there exist i1,i2 ∈ I such that j1 ∈ J with j1 < p(i1) (so (i1,j1) ∈ L) and j2 ∈ J with j2 > p(i2) (so (i2,j2) ∈ R). Since J is interval, it contains all columns between j1 and j2. Without loss of generality assume j1 ≤ j2 (if not, swap roles). Then J contains all columns c with j1 ≤ c ≤ j2. Since j1 < p(i1) and j2 > p(i2), we have p(i1) ∈ [j1, j2] or p(i2) ∈ [j1, j2] or both? Not necessarily. Let's examine possibilities:\n\nCase 1: p(i1) ≤ j2. Since j1 < p(i1) ≤ j2, p(i1) ∈ J. Then (i1,p(i1)) ∈ T, uncovered square. Contradiction.\n\nCase 2: p(i1) > j2. Then p(i1) > j2 > p(i2). So p(i2) < j2 < p(i1). Since j1 < p(i1) and j2 > p(i2), we have j1 ≤ j2. Now consider p(i2). Since p(i2) < j2, p(i2) may be ≤ j1? Possibly not. If p(i2) ≥ j1, then p(i2) ∈ J (since J includes all columns between j1 and j2). Then (i2,p(i2)) ∈ T, uncovered square. Contradiction. If p(i2) < j1, then p(i2) is not in J. So we have p(i2) < j1 < p(i1). So p(i2) < j1 < p(i1). But we also have j1 < p(i1). So j1 is between p(i2) and p(i1). But we need to check if p(i2) < j1 < p(i1). This is possible. In this scenario, J = [j1, j2] where j1 > p(i2) (since p(i2) < j1). So p(i2) is not in J. So no uncovered squares. But we need to check if j2 > p(i2) holds. Yes, j2 > p(i2). So rectangle contains a right cell (i2,j2). But we also need to ensure that j2 does not equal p(i1) (since p(i1) > j2). So j2 < p(i1). So J = [j1, j2] with p(i2) < j1 < j2 < p(i1). So J is strictly between p(i2) and p(i1). Then rectangle contains no uncovered squares. So this is a possible configuration: rectangle spans columns between p(i2)+1 and p(i1)-1 (if p(i2)+1 ≤ p(i1)-1). For rows i1,i2, we have left cell for i1 at column j1 (which is > p(i2) and < p(i1)), and right cell for i2 at column j2 (also between p(i2) and p(i1)). Both are left of p(i1) and right of p(i2). So rectangle lies entirely between the two holes p(i2) and p(i1). Does that intersect both L and R? For row i1, any column less than p(i1) is in L, so columns j1 and j2 are both less than p(i1), so they are in L for row i1. So (i1,j2) also in L. So rectangle does not contain any cell in R for row i1. For row i2, any column greater than p(i2) is in R, so columns j1 and j2 are greater than p(i2), so they are in R for row i2. So rectangle does not contain any cell in L for row i2. So rectangle intersects L for row i1 and R for row i2, but not both L and R for any single row. However, the definition of L and R is per cell, not per row. So rectangle contains cells that belong to L (for row i1) and cells that belong to R (for row i2). So rectangle intersects both L and R sets (as sets of cells). So Lemma 3 would be false if such rectangle is allowed. But is such rectangle allowed? Let's check constraints: The rectangle must be disjoint from uncovered squares. The rectangle we described is disjoint because J is strictly between p(i2) and p(i1), and p(i2) < p(i1). So J does not contain either p(i2) or p(i1). So rectangle does not contain uncovered squares. So it's allowed. So Lemma 3 is false as stated: there exist rectangles that intersect both L and R (across different rows) while not covering any uncovered squares.\n\nBut does the solution's later reasoning rely on Lemma 3 being true? They use it to separate rectangles into those wholly in L and wholly in R. If Lemma 3 is false, then some rectangles could be partially in L and partially in R, and the counting argument would need to handle them. However, they might still be able to bound the number of rectangles using a more refined argument, but they didn't. So their lower bound might be invalid if such rectangles exist because they could potentially reduce the number of rectangles needed.\n\nThus we need to examine if such rectangles could be used to reduce the number of tiles below 2n-2. The solution's lower bound argument uses left-corners and right-corners. If a rectangle can contain both a left-corner and a right-corner, then the counting argument would be weaker. But they also prove that each rectangle can contain at most one left-corner (Lemma 1) and at most one right-corner (Lemma 2). However, they didn't prove that a rectangle cannot contain both a left-corner and a right-corner. If a rectangle could contain both a left-corner and a right-corner, then the lower bound would be lower: we could have a rectangle that covers one left-corner and one right-corner, thus reducing the total number of rectangles needed. But does Lemma 1 and Lemma 2 together imply that a rectangle cannot contain both a left-corner and a right-corner? Let's examine.\n\nLemma 1: rectangle in L (i.e., entirely in L) contains at most one left-corner. Lemma 2: rectangle in R (entirely in R) con