ertical tile rows 1-4 column 5: (1,5),(2,5),(3,5),(4,5). That's allowed (includes right-corner (4,5)). So one tile.\n\nNow we have used the following tiles:\n\n- Tile A: vertical rows 3-5 col 1 (covers (3,1),(4,1),(5,1))\n- Tile B: vertical rows 2-5 col 2 (covers (2,2),(3,2),(4,2),(5,2))\n- Tile C: vertical rows 1-2 col 3 (covers (1,3),(2,3))\n- Tile D: vertical rows 4-5 col 3 (covers (4,3),(5,3))\n- Tile E: vertical rows 1-3 col 4 (covers (1,4),(2,4),(3,4))\n- Tile F: single cell (5,4) (left-corner)\n- Tile G: vertical rows 1-4 col 5 (covers (1,5),(2,5),(3,5),(4,5))\n\nNow count tiles: 7 tiles. Let's see if all cells are covered: Let's list all cells covered:\n\n- Row 1: (1,1) left-corner not covered yet. (1,3) covered by C, (1,4) covered by E, (1,5) covered by G. (1,2) hole. So (1,1) remains uncovered. We need to cover (1,1). Could we add a tile covering (1,1) alone? That's a 1x1 tile. That would be an 8th tile. But maybe we can incorporate (1,1) into some other tile.\n\nWe have tile A covering column 1 rows 3-5, tile B covering column 2 rows 2-5, tile C covering column 3 rows 1-2, tile D covering column 3 rows 4-5, tile E covering column 4 rows 1-3, tile G covering column 5 rows 1-4, tile F covering (5,4). So (1,1) is not covered. Could we adjust tile A to include row 1? No, because row 2 column 1 is a hole (2,1). So we cannot extend tile A upward beyond row 2. Could we create a horizontal tile covering (1,1) and maybe other cells? Row 1 left region is only column 1. So (1,1) cannot be combined horizontally with any other cell because column 2 is a hole. So (1,1) must be a separate tile (1x1). So we need an extra tile.\n\nThus total tiles = 8. That's same as 2n-2 = 8. So we didn't reduce tile count.\n\nBut perhaps we can find a different tiling that uses 7 tiles. Let's try to combine some of the vertical tiles we used into larger rectangles that cover more cells while still avoiding holes.\n\nThe challenge is that holes are at (1,2),(2,1),(3,3),(4,4),(5,5). The s