nd R, which are two monotone shapes: L is a shape formed by left of each hole, which is a \"lower-left\" shape that is not necessarily monotone? Let's examine shape L: For each row i, columns j < p(i) are included. This shape is a \"Ferrers board\" if the permutation is 132-avoiding? Not necessarily. Similarly R is shape of cells to the right.\n\nWe need to tile L ∪ R with rectangles that avoid holes. This is essentially a covering problem. The minimal number of rectangles needed to cover a Ferrers board (a Young diagram) is known to be equal to the number of rows (or columns) maybe? Actually covering a Ferrers board with rectangles (monotone shape) is related to the \"rectangle covering number\" which equals the size of a maximum antichain in the poset of cells? Not sure.\n\nBut the problem is more specific: we have two disjoint shapes L and R. Each shape is a \"skew shape\"? Actually L is a \"southwest\" shape: for each row i, columns 1..p(i)-1 are included. This shape is a \"left-justified\" shape: each row's left region is a prefix of columns. So L is a left-justified shape (Ferrers diagram) where row lengths are p(i)-1. The row lengths may not be monotone (they can go up and down). However, left-justified shape can be covered by rectangles in a certain way. The minimal number of rectangles needed to cover a left-justified shape is equal to the number of distinct row lengths? Actually there is known result: The rectangle covering number of a Young diagram equals the size of the largest antichain in the poset of cells (by Dilworth's theorem). For a Ferrers board, the largest antichain is the maximum number of cells no two of which are comparable in the partial order (i.e., not in same row or column). This is the size of a maximum matching? Actually the poset is defined by (i,j) ≤ (i',j') if i ≤ i' and j ≤ j'. An antichain is a set of cells where no one is ≤ another, i.e., they are in different rows and columns and not comparable. For a Ferrers shape, the size of 