joint and together cover all squares of \\(R\\).\n\nThus the whole board, except the diagonal holes, is tiled by  \n\n\\[\n(n-1)+(n-1)=2(n-1)\n\\]\n\nrectangular tiles.  Hence  \n\n\\[\n\\text{minimum number of tiles}\\le 2(n-1). \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  A lower bound for the left region  \n\nFix an arbitrary permutation \\(\\sigma\\).  \nFor each column \\(j\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) define  \n\n\\[\nS_j=\\{\\,i\\in[n]\\mid \\sigma(i)>j\\,\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe squares of column \\(j\\) belonging to \\(L\\) are exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\{(i,j):i\\in S_j\\}.\n\\]\n\nBecause a rectangle cannot contain a hole, any rectangle that contains a square of column \\(j\\) must have its **rightmost column** equal to \\(j\\).  \nConsequently such a rectangle can cover only a **contiguous block of rows** inside the set \\(S_j\\).  \n\nLet \\(I_j\\) be the number of maximal intervals (contiguous blocks) of the set \\(S_j\\).  \nEvery interval of \\(S_j\\) must be covered by at least one rectangle whose rightmost column is \\(j\\); therefore the number of rectangles whose rightmost column is \\(j\\) is at least \\(I_j\\).\n\nSince the permutation contains exactly \\(n-j\\) values larger than \\(j\\), the set \\(S_j\\) is non‑empty; hence \\(I_j\\ge1\\) for each \\(j\\).\n\nEvery rectangle covering the left region has a unique rightmost column, so the total number of rectangles covering \\(L\\) satisfies  \n\n\\[\n\\#\\text{tiles covering }L\\;\\ge\\;\\sum_{j=1}^{n-1} I_j\\;\\ge\\;\\sum_{j=1}^{n-1}1\\;=\\;n-1. \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  A lower bound for the right region  \n\nApply the same reasoning to the transpose of the board.  \nTransposing interchanges rows and columns and replaces the permutation \\(\\sigma\\) by its inverse \\(\\sigma^{-1}\\).  \nThe right region \\(R\\) of the original board becomes the left region of the transposed board, so (2) 