 S, not just the two squares from the same i. Did they prove that? Let's read the solution text:\n\n> *Counting.*  \n> The \\(2(n-1)\\) squares listed in (2) are all distinct: if \\((i,\\sigma(i+1))=(j+1,\\sigma(j))\\) then \\(i=j+1\\) and \\(\\sigma(i+1)=\\sigma(j)\\), which would imply \\(\\sigma(j+1)=\\sigma(j)\\), contradicting the injectivity of \\(\\sigma\\). Hence each of the \\(2(n-1)\\) squares must be covered by a different rectangle. Consequently any tiling of the board requires at least \\(2(n-1)\\) rectangles.\n\nThey assert that because the squares are distinct, each must be covered by a different rectangle. That's not logically valid: distinct squares could be covered by the same rectangle, as long as they are not prohibited by the earlier claim. The earlier claim only says a rectangle cannot contain both squares in a given pair (i). But they didn't prove that a rectangle cannot contain squares from different pairs. So the lower bound argument may be flawed.\n\nThus there might be a critical error in the reasoning. Let's examine more carefully: The set S includes 2(n-1) squares. For each i, we have two squares: s_i^{(1)} and s_i^{(2)}. The argument shows that a rectangle cannot contain both s_i^{(1)} and s_i^{(2)} because that would also contain holes (i,σ(i)) and (i+1,σ(i+1)). However, could a rectangle contain s_i^{(1)} and s_j^{(1)} for i≠j? Let's consider a rectangle that includes rows i and j+1 maybe? Actually s_i^{(1)} = (i,σ(i+1)). s_j^{(1)} = (j,σ(j+1)). For i≠j, these squares are in different rows and possibly different columns. Could a rectangle include both? If the rectangle's row set includes both i and j, and column set includes both σ(i+1) and σ(j+1), then the rectangle would also include the four corner squares: (i,σ(i+1)), (i,σ(j+1)), (j,σ(i+1)), (j,σ(j+1)). Among those, (i,σ(i+1)) and (j,σ(j+1)) are the squares we consider. The other two squares (i,σ(j+1)) and (j,σ(i+1)) may be holes? Let's see: The holes are (i,σ(i)) and (j,σ(j)). So