wer is 4048. Is that correct? Let's verify the problem: We need to find minimum number of tiles such that each row and each column has exactly one uncovered unit square. The solution suggests that the minimum is 2(n-1). Let's think if that is indeed minimal. The solution's lower bound argument seems plausible. Let's double-check the logic: For each i from 1 to n-1, they consider squares (i,σ(i+1)) and (i+1,σ(i)). Since σ is a permutation, σ(i) ≠ σ(i+1). So those squares are not holes. They claim a rectangle cannot contain both because then it would also contain the holes (i,σ(i)) and (i+1,σ(i+1)). Let's examine that claim: If a rectangle contains squares (i,σ(i+1)) and (i+1,σ(i)), then the rectangle's rows must include i and i+1, and its columns must include σ(i) and σ(i+1). But does it necessarily include the holes? The rectangle includes all squares whose row is between i and i+1 inclusive and column between σ(i) and σ(i+1) inclusive. So the rectangle includes the four corner squares: (i,σ(i)), (i,σ(i+1)), (i+1,σ(i)), (i+1,σ(i+1)). Indeed, the rectangle includes those four corners. Since (i,σ(i)) and (i+1,σ(i+1)) are holes, the rectangle would cover holes, which is not allowed. So a rectangle cannot contain both squares. So each rectangle can contain at most one of these 2(n-1) squares. Since each rectangle can contain at most one of these squares, we need at least 2(n-1) rectangles. So lower bound holds.\n\nThus the answer 4048 is correct.\n\nThus Level 1: CORRECT.\n\nLevel 2: Check reasoning validity. The solution uses valid methods: combinatorial reasoning, permutation representation, case analysis, counting argument, lower bound via counting distinct squares that must be in distinct rectangles. All methods are recognized valid mathematical methods. Also they provide a construction (explicit tiling) that is concrete: they define vertical rectangles V_j and horizontal rectangles H_i with explicit coordinates. This is a concrete specification, so Category C. So methods are valid. So Level 2: VALID.\n\nThus proceed to Level 3: Presentation quality. We need to examine any justification gaps or critical errors.\n\nPotential issues:\n\n- In the lower bound argument, they claim \"the 2(n-1) squares listed in (2) are all distinct\". They give a brief argument: if (i,σ(i+1)) = (j+1,σ(j)), then i=j+1 and σ(i+1)=σ(j), which would imply σ(j+1)=σ(j), contradicting injectivity. That seems correct. They may have a minor gap: they need to also consider the case where (i,σ(i+1)) = (i+1,σ(i))? But those are different squares because σ(i) ≠ σ(i+1). So they are distinct. The argument is fine.\n\n- The solution uses a phrase \"Because σ is a permutation, σ(i)≠σ(i+1)\". That's correct.\n\n- The solution says \"the set of rows of R must contain both i and i+1, and the set of columns of R must contain both σ(i) and σ(i+1)\". That's correct for a rectangle containing those two squares. Then they claim \"Consequently R also contains the four corner squares\". That's correct.\n\n- The solution's construction uses vertical rectangles V_j = {(i,j) | i=j+1,...,n} and horizontal rectangles H_i = {(i,j) | j=i+1,...,n}. They claim these are pairwise disjoint and cover all squares left/right of diagonal. That's correct.\n\n- The solution uses \"the whole board, except the diagonal holes, is tiled by (n-1)+(n-1) rectangular tiles\". That's correct.\n\nThus the solution appears rigorous and complete. There may be minor presentation issues: they could have been more explicit about why the rectangles are disjoint, but it's clear. They could have clarified that the vertical rectangles are of width 1 and height n-j, and horizontal rectangles of height 1 and width n-i. That's fine.\n\nThus no critical errors. Possibly there are justification gaps: maybe they didn't explicitly prove that the vertical rectangles V_j and horizontal rectangles H_i are indeed rectangles (they are sets of unit squares forming a rectangle shape). But it's clear. Also they didn't explic