he \\(w\\)-values of the rows it contains.  \n\nNow look at the cell \\((i,w_i)\\) – the rightmost cell of row \\(i\\) in \\(L\\).  \nIt belongs to a unique rectangle of the partition; that rectangle’s rightmost column is the minimum \\(w\\)-value among its rows, and therefore equals \\(w_j\\) for the (unique) row \\(j\\) that attains this minimum.  \nHence each rectangle can be associated with a *distinct* row \\(j\\) for which \\(w_j\\) is the smallest among the rows of that rectangle.\n\nSince the numbers \\(w_1,\\dots ,w_n\\) are all different, the rows that can serve as such minima are exactly those with \\(w_i>0\\) (the row with \\(w_i=0\\) has no cell in \\(L\\) at all).  \nThere are \\(n-1\\) rows with \\(w_i>0\\); each of them must be the minimal‑\\(w\\) row of a different rectangle.  \nTherefore any partition of \\(L\\) uses at least \\(n-1\\) rectangles:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{|\\,\\text{rectangles covering }L\\,|\\ge n-1}.\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  A lower bound for the right region  \n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\nr_i:=n-\\pi(i)\\qquad(0\\le r_i\\le n-1).\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nThe set \\(R\\) consists of the cells \\((i,j)\\) with \\(n-r_i+1\\le j\\le n\\); after reflecting the picture horizontally, \\(R\\) becomes a histogram of heights \\(r_1,\\dots ,r_n\\).  \nThe same argument as in §1 (applied to the reflected picture) shows that any partition of \\(R\\) requires at least \\(n-1\\) rectangles:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{|\\,\\text{rectangles covering }R\\,|\\ge n-1}.\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Global lower bound  \n\nEvery tile of the original board lies wholly in \\(L\\) or wholly in \\(R\\); consequently the total number \\(T\\) of tiles satisfies  \n\n\\[\nT\\ge (n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2.\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nFor \\(n=2025\\) this gives  \n\n\\[\nT\\ge 2\\cdot2025-2=4048 .\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  A construction attaining the bound  \n\nTake the identity permutation \\(\\pi(i)=i\\) (the uncovered squares are the main diagonal).  \nThen  \n\n\\[\nL=\\{(i,j)\\mid j<i\\},\\qquad \nR=\\{(i,j)\\mid j>i\\}.\n\\tag{10}\n\\]\n\n*Left region.* For each \\(k=2,\\dots ,n\\) define  \n\n\\[\nL_k:=\\{(i,j)\\mid k\\le i\\le n,\\;1\\le j\\le k-1\\}.\n\\tag{11}\n\\]\n\n\\(L_k\\) is a rectangle of size \\((n-k+1)\\times(k-1)\\).  \nThe sets \\(L_2,\\dots ,L_n\\) are pairwise disjoint and their union equals \\(L\\).  \nThus \\(L\\) is tiled by exactly \\(n-1\\) rectangles.\n\n*Right region.* For each \\(k=1,\\dots ,n-1\\) define  \n\n\\[\nR_k:=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le i\\le k,\\;k+1\\le j\\le n\\}.\n\\tag{12}\n\\]\n\n\\(R_k\\) is a rectangle of size \\(k\\times(n-k)\\).  \nThe sets \\(R_1,\\dots ,R_{n-1}\\) are pairwise disjoint and their union equals \\(R\\).  \nHence \\(R\\) is tiled by exactly \\(n-1\\) rectangles.\n\nTogether the \\(2n-2\\) rectangles \\(\\{L_k\\}_{k=2}^n\\cup\\{R_k\\}_{k=1}^{n-1}\\) cover every unit square except the diagonal holes, and each row and each column contains precisely one uncovered square.  \n\nTherefore a tiling with \\(2n-2\\) tiles exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  Conclusion  \n\nThe lower bound (8) and the explicit construction (Section 4) show that the minimal possible number of tiles equals \\(2n-2\\).  \nFor the given board with \\(n=2025\\),\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{4048}\n\\]\n\ntiles are necessary and sufficient."
