_1\\); since \\(j_2>j_1\\), the square \\((q(j_1),j_2)\\) lies to the right of the hole, i.e. in \\(N\\).  \n   This contradicts \\(R\\subseteq S\\).  \n   Hence any rectangle contained in \\(S\\) can contain **at most one** corner cell of \\(S\\); the same holds for rectangles in \\(N\\).\n\n5. **Lower bound.**  \n   Because \\(S\\) has exactly \\(n-1\\) corner cells and each tile covering a part of \\(S\\) can contain at most one, at least \\(n-1\\) tiles are needed for \\(S\\).  \n   Similarly, at least \\(n-1\\) tiles are needed for \\(N\\).  \n   Therefore any admissible tiling uses at least  \n\n   \\[\n   (n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2\n   \\]\n\n   tiles.\n\n6. **Construction attaining the bound.**  \n   Choose the identity permutation \\(p(i)=i\\); the uncovered squares are the diagonal cells \\((i,i)\\).  \n   *For the south\u2011west region* (\\(j<i\\)): for each column \\(j\\) with \\(1\\le j\\le n-1\\) place the vertical tile  \n\n   \\[\n   V_j=\\{(i,j)\\mid i>j\\},\n   \\]\n\n   covering all squares strictly below the diagonal in column \\(j\\).  \n   *For the north\u2011east region* (\\(j>i\\)): for each row \\(i\\) with \\(1\\le i\\le n-1\\) place the horizontal tile  \n\n   \\[\n   H_i=\\{(i,j)\\mid j>i\\},\n   \\]\n\n   covering all squares strictly above the diagonal in row \\(i\\).  \n\n   The \\(n-1\\) vertical tiles are pairwise disjoint, the \\(n-1\\) horizontal tiles are pairwise disjoint, and the two families are disjoint from each other.  \n   Together they cover every square except the diagonal holes, using exactly \\(2n-2\\) tiles.\n\n7. **Conclusion.**  \n   The lower bound \\(2n-2\\) and the explicit construction with \\(2n-2\\) tiles coincide; thus the minimum possible number of tiles is \\(2n-2\\).  \n   For \\(n=2025\\),\n\n   \\[\n   2\\cdot2025-2 = 4048.\n   \\]\n\n   Hence the answer is \\(\\boxed{4048}\\).\n\n---\n\n### Detailed Solution ###\n\nLet \\(n=2025\\).  Denote the set of unit squares of the board by  \n\n\\[\nB=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le i\\le n,\\;1\\le j\\le n\\}.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  Uncovered squares form a permutation matrix  \n\nSince each row and each column must contain exactly one uncovered unit square, there exists a bijection  \n\n\\[\np:\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\longrightarrow\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\n\\]\n\nsuch that the unique uncovered square in row \\(i\\) is \\((i,p(i))\\).  \nWrite \\(q=p^{-1}\\); thus \\(q(j)\\) is the row whose uncovered square lies in column \\(j\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  The monotone separating path  \n\nConsider the lattice points  \n\n\\[\n(0,n),\\;(0,p(1)),\\;(1,p(1)),\\;(1,p(2)),\\;(2,p(2)),\\dots ,\\;(n-1,p(n)),\\;(n,p(n))=(n,0).\n\\]\n\nJoining consecutive points by horizontal or vertical unit segments yields a lattice path \\(P\\) that\n\n* starts at \\((0,n)\\) and ends at \\((n,0)\\);\n* consists of \\(n\\) horizontal and \\(n\\) vertical unit segments, alternating;\n* passes through every hole \\((i,p(i))\\).\n\nThe path partitions the board into two disjoint subsets:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nS &=\\{(i,j)\\in B\\mid j<p(i)\\text{ and }i>q(j)\\},\\\\\nN &=\\{(i,j)\\in B\\mid j>p(i)\\text{ and }i<q(j)\\}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nThus every covered square belongs either to \\(S\\) (south\u2011west of \\(P\\)) or to \\(N\\) (north\u2011east of \\(P\\)).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Every tile lies wholly in one region  \n\nLet \\(R\\) be a tile, i.e. an axis\u2011parallel rectangle whose interior consists only of covered squares.  \nAssume, for contradiction, that \\(R\\) contains squares from both \\(S\\) and \\(N\\).  \nThen there exists a row index \\(i\\) such that \\(R\\) contains a square \\((i,a)\\) with \\(a<p(i)\\) (hence in \\(S\\)) and a square \\((i,b)\\) with \\(b>p(i)\\) (hence in \\(N\\)).  \nSince \\(R\\) is a rectangle, it contains all squares \\((i,c)\\) for \\(a\\le c\\le b\\), in particular the hole \\((i,p(i))\\).  \nThis contradicts the requirement that holes remain uncovered.  \nTherefore each tile is contained entirely in \\(S\\) or entirely in \\(N\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Corner cells of the regions  \n\nThe path \\(P\\) has \\(n\\) horizontal segments.  \nFor each horizontal segment except the first (the one on the top border), the unit square immediately to its left lies inside the board and belongs to \\(S\\).  \nThese \\(n-1\\) squares are the *corner cells* of \\(S\\); they can be written as  \n\n\\[\nc_j=(\\,q(j),\\,j\\,),\\qquad j=1,2,\\dots ,n-1,\n\\]\n\nbecause the horizontal segment at height \\(j\\) ends at the point \\((q(j),j)\\).\n\nSimilarly, the \\(n-1\\) vertical segments of \\(P\\) (all except the last) have a unit square immediately above them that lies in \\(N\\); these are the corner cells of \\(N\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  At most one corner cell per rectangle (contradiction proof)  \n\nConsider a rectangle \\(R\\subseteq S\\) that contains two distinct corner cells \\(c_{j_1}\\) and \\(c_{j_2}\\) with \\(j_1<j_2\\).  \nWrite \\(c_{j_1}=(q(j_1),j_1)\\) and \\(c_{j_2}=(q(j_2),j_2)\\).  \nSince \\(R\\) is axis\u2011parallel, it contains the whole Cartesian product  \n\n\\[\n\\{q(j_1),\\dots ,q(j_2)\\}\\times\\{j_1,\\dots ,j_2\\}.\n\\]\n\nHence the square \\((q(j_1),j_2)\\) belongs to \\(R\\).  \nIn row \\(q(j_1)\\) the uncovered square is at column \\(j_1\\) (because \\(p(q(j_1))=j_1\\)).  \nSince \\(j_2>j_1\\), the square \\((q(j_1),j_2)\\) lies to the right of that hole, i.e. it belongs to the north\u2011east region \\(N\\).  \nThus \\(R\\) cannot be a subset of \\(S\\), a contradiction.  \n\nConsequently any rectangle contained in \\(S\\) can contain **at most one** corner cell of \\(S\\).  \nThe same argument, with the roles of rows and columns interchanged, shows that any rectangle contained in \\(N\\) can contain at most one corner cell of \\(N\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 6.  Lower bound on the number of tiles  \n\nRegion \\(S\\) possesses exactly \\(n-1\\) corner cells, each of which must be covered by some tile.  \nSince a tile can cover at most one such cell, at least \\(n-1\\) tiles are required for \\(S\\).  \nAnalogously, at least \\(n-1\\) tiles are required for \\(N\\).  \n\nTherefore any admissible tiling of the whole board uses at least  \n\n\\[\n(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2\n\\]\n\ntiles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 7.  Construction achieving the bound  \n\nChoose the identity permutation \\(p(i)=i\\) for all \\(i\\).  \nThe uncovered squares are precisely the diagonal cells \\((i,i)\\).\n\n*For the south\u2011west region.*  \nFor each column \\(j\\) with \\(1\\le j\\le n-1\\) place the vertical tile  \n\n\\[\nV_j=\\{(i,j)\\mid i>j\\}.\n\\]\n\n\\(V_j\\) consists of all squares strictly below the diagonal in column \\(j\\); it contains no diagonal cell, so it is a legal tile.\n\n*For the north\u2011east region.*  \nFor each row \\(i\\) with \\(1\\le i\\le n-1\\) place the horizontal tile  \n\n\\[\nH_i=\\{(i,j)\\mid j>i\\}.\n\\]\n\n\\(H_i\\) consists of all squares strictly above the diagonal in row \\(i\\); it also avoids every diagonal cell.\n\nThe sets \\(\\{V_j\\}_{j=1}^{n-1}\\) and \\(\\{H_i\\}_{i=1}^{n-1}\\) are pairwise disjoint, and together they cover every non\u2011diagonal square of the board.  \nThus we obtain a tiling with exactly  \n\n\\[\n(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2\n\\]\n\ntiles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 8.  Conclusion  \n\nThe lower bound \\(2n-2\\) and the explicit tiling with \\(2n-2\\) tiles coincide; therefore the minimum possible number of tiles is  \n\n\\[\n2n-2.\n\\]\n\nFor the given board \\(n=2025\\),\n\n\\[\n2\\cdot2025-2 = 4048.\n\\]\n\nHence the answer is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{4048}.\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### Self-Correction Instruction ###\n\nBefore finalizing your output, carefully review your \"Summary\" and \"Detailed Solution\" sections to ensure they are clean, rigorous, and strictly adhere to all instructions provided above. Verify that every statement contributes directly to the final, coherent mathematical argument."
