s tile is a \\(1\\times(2025-i)\\) rectangle.  \n   – For each column \\(j\\) with \\(1\\le j\\le 2024\\) put a vertical tile covering the squares \\((j+1,j),(j+2,j),\\dots ,(2025,j)\\); this tile is a \\((2025-j)\\times1\\) rectangle.  \n\n   The two families of tiles are disjoint, they cover every square except the diagonal ones, and there are exactly \\(2024+2024=4048\\) tiles.  \n   Each row and each column contains precisely its diagonal square as the unique uncovered cell, satisfying the problem’s requirement.\n\nSince the lower bound equals the number of tiles in the explicit construction, the minimum possible number of tiles is \\(4048\\).\n\n---\n\n**### Detailed Solution ###**\n\nLet \\(n=2025\\).  \nA placement of uncovered squares satisfying “one per row and one per column’’ is a permutation matrix: there exists a permutation \\(\\sigma\\) of \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) such that the uncovered squares are exactly  \n\n\\[\nU=\\{(i,\\sigma(i))\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 1 (Reduction to the diagonal).  \nFor any permutation \\(\\sigma\\) there is a bijection between tilings of the board with uncovered set \\(U\\) and tilings with uncovered set \\(\\{(i,i)\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) that preserves the number of tiles.\n\n*Proof.*  \nApply the column permutation \\(\\pi=\\sigma^{-1}\\).  \nA square \\((i,j)\\) is sent to \\((i,\\pi(j))\\); in particular \\((i,\\sigma(i))\\) is sent to \\((i,i)\\).  \nSince a rectangle is defined by two intervals of column indices, the image of a rectangle under \\(\\pi\\) is again a rectangle of the same size, and rectangles remain disjoint.  \nThus a tiling for \\(U\\) is transformed into a tiling for the diagonal with the same number of rectangles, and the process is reversible. ∎\n\nHence we may assume from now on that the uncovered squares are the diagonal cells  \n\n\\[\nD=\\{(i,i)\\mid 1\\le i\\le n\\}.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 2 (Rectangles stay on one side of the diagonal).  \nLet \\(R\\) be a rectangle whose interior contains no cell of \\(D\\). Then either every cell of \\(R\\) satisfies row < column (the rectangle lies strictly above the diagonal) or every cell satisfies row > column (the rectangle lies strictly below the diagonal).\n\n*Proof.*  \nWrite \\(R=[a,b]\\times[c,d]\\) with \\(a\\le b\\) and \\(c\\le d\\).  \nIf there exist \\((i,j)\\in R\\) with \\(i<j\\) and \\((i',j')\\in R\\) with \\(i'>j'\\), then \\(a\\le i\\le b\\), \\(c\\le j\\le d\\) and \\(a\\le i'\\le b\\), \\(c\\le j'\\le d\\).  \nConsequently the intervals \\([a,b]\\) and \\([c,d]\\) intersect, so there exists \\(k\\in[a,b]\\cap[c,d]\\).  \nThe cell \\((k,k)\\) would belong to \\(R\\), contradicting the hypothesis that \\(R\\) avoids \\(D\\).  \nThus such a pair cannot exist, and all cells of \\(R\\) lie on the same side of the diagonal. ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 3 (Upper‑half bound).  \nConsider the set  \n\n\\[\nC^{+}=\\{(i,i+1)\\mid 1\\le i\\le n-1\\},\n\\]\n\nthe cells immediately to the right of the diagonal.  \nIn any partition of the region  \n\n\\[\nU^{+}=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le i<j\\le n\\}\n\\]\n\ninto rectangles, each rectangle contains at most one element of \\(C^{+}\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nLet a rectangle \\(R=[a,b]\\times[c,d]\\) lie in \\(U^{+}\\).  \nBy Lemma 2 we have \\(b<c\\).  \nIf \\((i,i+1)\\in R\\), then \\(a\\le i\\le b\\) and \\(c\\le i+1\\le d\\).  \nBecause \\(b<c\\) and \\(i+1\\ge c\\), we must have \\(b=i\\) and \\(c=i+1\\); otherwise either \\(i\\notin[a,b]\\) or \\(i+1\\notin[c,d]\\).  \nThus the row interval of \\(R\\) ends at \\(i\\) and the column interval starts at \\(i+1\\).  \nFor any \\(i'\\neq i\\) we cannot have both \\(i'\\in[a,b]\\) and \\(i'+1\\in[c,d]\\):  \nif \\(i'<i\\) then \\(i'+1\\le i<c\\); if \\(i'>i\\) then \\(i'>b\\).  \nHence no other cell of \\(C^{+}\\) lies