ctly \\(N\\) tiles match, so the minimum possible number of tiles is \\(N=2025\\).\n\n---\n\n### Detailed Solution ###\n\nLet \\(N=2025\\).  \nDenote the unit squares of the board by ordered pairs \\((i,j)\\) with \\(1\\le i,j\\le N\\) (row \\(i\\), column \\(j\\)).\n\n---\n\n#### 1.  Structure of the uncovered squares  \n\nBecause each row contains exactly one uncovered square and each column contains exactly one uncovered square, the uncovered squares must lie in distinct rows and distinct columns.  \nHence there exists a permutation \\(\\pi\\) of \\(\\{1,\\dots ,N\\}\\) such that the uncovered squares are precisely  \n\n\\[\nU=\\{(i,\\pi(i))\\mid i=1,\\dots ,N\\}.\n\\]\n\nLet \\(P\\) be the \\(N\\times N\\) permutation matrix of \\(\\pi\\); i.e. \\(p_{i,j}=1\\) iff \\(j=\\pi(i)\\) and \\(p_{i,j}=0\\) otherwise.\n\n---\n\n#### 2.  Matrix of the covered squares  \n\nDefine the matrix  \n\n\\[\nA=(a_{i,j})_{1\\le i,j\\le N},\\qquad \na_{i,j}= \n\\begin{cases}\n0,& (i,j)\\in U,\\\\[2pt]\n1,& \\text{otherwise}.\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\n\nThus \\(A\\) has a zero exactly at the positions of the uncovered squares and a one elsewhere.  \nConsequently  \n\n\\[\nA=J-P,\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(J\\) is the all‑ones matrix (every entry equal to \\(1\\)).\n\n---\n\n#### 3.  Upper bound: a tiling with \\(N\\) tiles  \n\nChoose the permutation \\(\\pi\\) to be the identity, i.e. \\(\\pi(i)=i\\) for all \\(i\\).  \nThen the uncovered squares are the diagonal squares \\((i,i)\\).\n\nFor each column \\(j\\) (\\(1\\le j\\le N\\)) place a single rectangular tile covering all squares of column \\(j\\) except the diagonal square \\((j,j)\\).  \nEach such tile is a \\((N-1)\\times1\\) rectangle; its sides lie on grid lines, the tiles are pairwise disjoint, and together they cover every square whose entry in \\(A\\) equals \\(1\\).\n\nThus we have exhibited a tiling using exactly \\(N\\) tiles. Hence\n\n\\[\n\\text{minimum number of tiles}\\le N .\n\\]\n\n---\n\n#### 4.  Lower bound via rank  \n\nEach tile is a rectangle \\(R\\times C\\) where \\(R\\subseteq\\{1,\\dots ,N\\}\\) is a non‑empty set of rows and \\(C\\subseteq\\{1,\\dots ,N\\}\\) a non‑empty set of columns, with the property that \\((i,\\pi(i))\\notin R\\times C\\) for any \\(i\\).  \n\nLet \\(u\\in\\{0,1\\}^{N}\\) be the characteristic vector of \\(R\\) (\\(u_i=1\\) iff \\(i\\in R\\)) and \\(v\\in\\{0,1\\}^{N}\\) the characteristic vector of \\(C\\).  \nThe indicator matrix of the tile equals the outer product \\(uv^{\\mathsf T}\\); therefore it has rank \\(1\\) (both \\(u\\) and \\(v\\) are non‑zero).\n\nSince the tiles partition the set of covered squares, the matrix \\(A\\) is the sum of the indicator matrices of all tiles.  \nIf the tiling uses \\(t\\) tiles, we have\n\n\\[\nA=\\sum_{k=1}^{t}u^{(k)}\\bigl(v^{(k)}\\bigr)^{\\mathsf T},\n\\]\n\nhence\n\n\\[\n\\operatorname{rank}(A)\\le\\sum_{k=1}^{t}\\operatorname{rank}\\bigl(u^{(k)}(v^{(k)})^{\\mathsf T}\\bigr)\n= \\sum_{k=1}^{t}1 = t .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nThus \\(t\\ge\\operatorname{rank}(A)\\).\n\n---\n\n#### 5.  Computing \\(\\operatorname{rank}(A)\\)\n\nRecall \\(A=J-P\\).  \nWe claim that \\(J-P\\) is invertible, whence \\(\\operatorname{rank}(A)=N\\).\n\nSuppose \\((J-P)x=0\\) for some vector \\(x\\in\\mathbb{R}^{N}\\).  \nLet \\(s=\\sum_{i=1}^{N}x_i\\). Then \\(Jx = s\\mathbf{1}\\), where \\(\\mathbf{1}=(1,\\dots ,1)^{\\mathsf T}\\).  \nThe equation \\((J-P)x=0\\) becomes\n\n\\[\ns\\mathbf{1}=Px .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(P\\) merely permutes the coordinates of \\(x\\), the right‑hand side of (2) is a permutation of the entries of \\(x\\).  \nEquation (2) therefore forces all coordinates of \\(x\\) to be equal: there exists a constant \\(c\\) with \\(x_i=c\\) for every \\(i\\).  \nConsequently \\(s=Nc\\) and (2) reads \\(Nc\\mathbf{1}=c\\mathbf{1}\\), i.e. \\((N-1)c\\mathbf{1}=0\\).  \nBecause \\(N>1\\), we obtain \\(c=0\\) and thus \\(x=0\\).\n\nHence the kernel of \\(J-P\\) is trivial; \\(J-P\\) is nonsingular and \\(\\operatorname{rank