le is a rectangle \\(R\\times C\\) where \\(R\\subseteq\\{1,\\dots ,N\\}\\) is a non‑empty set of rows and \\(C\\subseteq\\{1,\\dots ,N\\}\\) a non‑empty set of columns, with the property that \\((i,\\pi(i))\\notin R\\times C\\) for any \\(i\\).  \n\nLet \\(u\\in\\{0,1\\}^{N}\\) be the characteristic vector of \\(R\\) (\\(u_i=1\\) iff \\(i\\in R\\)) and \\(v\\in\\{0,1\\}^{N}\\) the characteristic vector of \\(C\\).  \nThe indicator matrix of the tile equals the outer product \\(uv^{\\mathsf T}\\); therefore it has rank \\(1\\) (both \\(u\\) and \\(v\\) are non‑zero).\n\nSince the tiles partition the set of covered squares, the matrix \\(A\\) is the sum of the indicator matrices of all tiles.  \nIf the tiling uses \\(t\\) tiles, we have\n\n\\[\nA=\\sum_{k=1}^{t}u^{(k)}\\bigl(v^{(k)}\\bigr)^{\\mathsf T},\n\\]\n\nhence\n\n\\[\n\\operatorname{rank}(A)\\le\\sum_{k=1}^{t}\\operatorname{rank}\\bigl(u^{(k)}(v^{(k)})^{\\mathsf T}\\bigr)\n= \\sum_{k=1}^{t}1 = t .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nThus \\(t\\ge\\operatorname{rank}(A)\\).\n\n---\n\n#### 5.  Computing \\(\\operatorname{rank}(A)\\)\n\nRecall \\(A=J-P\\).  \nWe claim that \\(J-P\\) is invertible, whence \\(\\operatorname{rank}(A)=N\\).\n\nSuppose \\((J-P)x=0\\) for some vector \\(x\\in\\mathbb{R}^{N}\\).  \nLet \\(s=\\sum_{i=1}^{N}x_i\\). Then \\(Jx = s\\mathbf{1}\\), where \\(\\mathbf{1}=(1,\\dots ,1)^{\\mathsf T}\\).  \nThe equation \\((J-P)x=0\\) becomes\n\n\\[\ns\\mathbf{1}=Px .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(P\\) merely permutes the coordinates of \\(x\\), the right‑hand side of (2) is a permutation of the entries of \\(x\\).  \nEquation (2) therefore forces all coordinates of \\(x\\) to be equal: there exists a constant \\(c\\) with \\(x_i=c\\) for every \\(i\\).  \nConsequently \\(s=Nc\\) and (2) reads \\(Nc\\mathbf{1}=c\\mathbf{1}\\), i.e. \\((N-1)c\\mathbf{1}=0\\).  \nBecause \\(N>1\\), we obtain \\(c=0\\) and thus \\(x=0\\).\n\nHence the kernel of \\(J-P\\) is trivial; \\(J-P\\) is nonsingular and \\(\\operatorname{rank}(J-