xcept those N holes? Equivalent to partitioning the complement of a permutation matrix into rectangles.\n\nWe need to find the minimal number of rectangles needed to partition the complement of a permutation matrix (i.e., the board minus N cells, one per row and column). The complement is a set of N^2 - N squares. We can partition them into axis-aligned rectangles. The question: what is the minimal number of rectangles needed for any permutation? Or maybe we can choose the permutation to minimize the number of rectangles needed? The problem statement: \"Matilda wishes to place on the grid some rectangular tiles, possibly of different sizes, such that each side of every tile lies on a grid line and every unit square is covered by at most one tile. Determine the minimum number of tiles Matilda needs to place so that each row and each column of the grid has exactly one unit square that is not covered by any tile.\" It does not specify that the uncovered squares must be placed arbitrarily; we can choose which squares are uncovered. So the problem is to find the minimal number of tiles needed to achieve the condition, i.e., we can choose the permutation that yields the minimal number of rectangles needed to cover the rest.\n\nThus we need to find the minimum over all permutations of the minimal rectangle partition of the complement. The solution chooses an arbitrary permutation, but then uses a lower bound that applies to any permutation? Actually, the lemma applied to the right side after sorting rows by increasing hole column yields a Young diagram with row lengths N-π(i). Since π(i) is a permutation of {1,...,N}, the lengths N-π(i) are a permutation of {0,1,...,N-1}. After sorting rows by increasing π(i), the lengths become N-1,N-2,...,0. So the shape is always the same: a Young diagram with distinct row lengths. So the lower bound of N-1 rectangles for covering the right side region holds for any permutation. Similarly for left side region (the lengths π(i)-1). After