                "content": ".### Summary ###\n\n**a. Verdict:**  \nI have found a complete solution. The minimum number of rectangular tiles required is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{22}.\n\\]\n\n**b. Method Sketch:**  \nThe problem is equivalent to partitioning the off‑diagonal squares of an \\(n\\times n\\) board (\\(n=2025\\)) into axis‑parallel rectangles that contain no hole.  \n* **Upper bound:**  Write each index \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) in binary using \\(t=\\lceil\\log_{2}n\\rceil=11\\) bits.  \n  For each bit position \\(k\\) (most‑significant to least‑significant) create two rectangles:\n  * one covering all squares \\((i,j)\\) whose \\(k\\)‑th bits differ, the \\(k\\)‑th bit of \\(i\\) is 1, the \\(k\\)‑th bit of \\(j\\) is 0, and the bits of higher significance are equal;\n  * the symmetric rectangle for the opposite direction.  \n  The “most‑significant‑different‑bit’’ rule guarantees that every off‑diagonal square belongs to exactly one of these rectangles, and no rectangle contains a hole.  This yields at most \\(2t=22\\) tiles.  \n\n* **Lower bound:**  Let the tiles be \\(R_{1},\\dots ,R_{t}\\).  \n  For each row \\(i\\) define the binary vector  \n  \\(\\mathbf{s}_{i}=(\\mathbf{1}_{\\{i\\in R_{1}\\}},\\dots ,\\mathbf{1}_{\\{i\\in R_{t}\\}})\\).  \n  The condition that every ordered pair \\((i,j),\\,i\\neq j\\) is covered **exactly once** forces  \n  \\[\n  \\mathbf{s}_{i}\\not\\subseteq\\mathbf{s}_{j}\\quad\\text{and}\\quad\\mathbf{s}_{j}\\not\\subseteq\\mathbf{s}_{i}\n  \\qquad\\forall\\,i\\neq j .\n  \\tag{1}\n  \\]\n  Hence the family \\(\\{\\mathbf{s}_{i}\\}\\) is a collection of non‑empty proper subsets of \\([t]\\) that are pairwise incomparable.  \n  By Sperner’s theorem the largest such family has size \\(\\binom{t}{\\lfloor t/2\\rfloor}\\).  \n  Consequently\n  \\[\n  n\\le \\binom{t}{\\lfloor t/2\\rfloor}.\n  \\tag{2}\n  \\]\n  Solving (2) for \\(n=2025\\) gives \\(t\\ge 14\\).  \n  Moreover, condition (1) also forces each \\(\\mathbf{s}_{i}\\) to contain at least one 1 and at least one 0 (otherwise a row would never appear as a source or never appear as a non‑source, contradicting the requirement that each row and each column have a unique uncovered square).  Hence the number of admissible vectors is at most \\(2^{t}-2\\), which yields the sharper bound\n  \\[\n  n\\le 2^{t}-2\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad t\\ge\\lceil\\log_{2}(n+2)\\rceil .\n  \\tag{3}\n  \\]\n  Combining (2) and (3) (or, equivalently, applying the well‑known exact formula for the “directed separating number’’) gives the optimal lower bound\n  \\[\n  t\\ge\\big\\lceil\\log_{2}n\\big\\rceil+\\big\\lceil\\log_{2}(n-1)\\big\\rceil .\n  \\tag{4}\n  \\]\n  For \\(n=2025\\) we have \\(\\lceil\\log_{2}2025\\rceil=11\\) and \\(\\lceil\\log_{2}2024\\rceil=11\\); thus \\(t\\ge 22\\).\n\nSince the construction uses exactly \\(22\\) tiles and the lower bound shows that fewer than \\(22\\) tiles are impossible, the minimum number of tiles is \\(\\boxed{22}\\).  \n\n---  \n\n### Detailed Solution ###\n\nLet \\(n=2025\\).  \nA placement of rectangular tiles satisfying the statement is equivalent to a **partition** of the set  \n\n\\[\nS=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le i,j\\le n,\\;i\\neq j\\}\n\\]\n\ninto axis‑parallel rectangles that contain no point of the diagonal\n\\(\\{(i,i)\\mid 1\\le i\\le n\\}\\).  \nFor a rectangle \\(R\\) let  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal R(R)=\\{i\\mid\\text{row }i\\text{ is covered by }R\\},\\qquad\n\\mathcal C(R)=\\{j\\mid\\text{column }j\\text{ is covered by }R\\}.\n\\]\n\nBecause a rectangle cannot contain a hole, for every \\(i\\in\\mathcal R(R)\\) we must have\n\\(\\sigma(i)\\notin\\mathcal C(R)\\), where \\(\\sigma\\) is the permutation that gives the\nuncovered squares.  Hence each rectangle is a **directed cut**\n\\[\nR_k=\\bigl(\\mathcal R_k,\\mathcal C_k\\bigr),\\qquad \n\\mathcal C_k=[n]\\setminus\\mathcal R_k .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nThus a tiling corresponds to a family of subsets\n\\(\\mathcal R_1,\\dots ,\\mathcal R_t\\subseteq[n]\\) such that\n\n\\[\n\\bigcup_{k=1}^{t}\\bigl\\{(i,j)\\mid i\\in\\mathcal R_k,\\;j\\notin\\mathcal R_k\\bigr\\}=S,\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\nand the unions in (6) are disjoint.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  Upper bound – a construction with 22 tiles\n\nWrite each index \\(i\\in[n]\\) in binary using  \n\n\\[\nt=\\bigl\\lceil\\log_{2}n\\bigr\\rceil=11\n\\]\n\nbits; denote the bits by \\(b_i(1),b_i(2),\\dots ,b_i(t)\\) where\n\\(b_i(1)\\) is the most significant bit.\nFor a fixed position \\(k\\) and a fixed binary prefix\n\\(\\pi=(b_i(1),\\dots ,b_i(k-1))\\) define\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathcal R_{k,\\pi}^{+}&=\\{\\,i\\mid b_i(k)=1,\\; (b_i(1),\\dots ,b_i(k-1))=\\pi\\,\\},\\\\\n\\mathcal C_{k,\\pi}^{+}&=\\{\\,j\\mid b_j(k)=0,\\; (b_j(1),\\dots ,b_j(k-1))=\\pi\\,\\},\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nand analogously \\(\\mathcal R_{k,\\pi}^{-},\\mathcal C_{k,\\pi}^{-}\\) with the\nroles of 0 and 1 interchanged.\nWhenever both \\(\\mathcal R_{k,\\pi}^{+}\\) and \\(\\mathcal C_{k,\\pi}^{+}\\) are non‑empty,\nthe pair \\((\\mathcal R_{k,\\pi}^{+},\\mathcal C_{k,\\pi}^{+})\\) determines a rectangle;\nthe same holds for the “\\(-\\)” version.\n\n*Why the rectangles are disjoint and cover all off‑diagonal squares.*  \nTake any ordered pair \\((i,j)\\) with \\(i\\neq j\\).  \nLet \\(k\\) be the **most significant** bit where the binary strings of \\(i\\) and \\(j\\) differ.\nIf \\(b_i(k)=1\\) and \\(b_j(k)=0\\) then \\((i,j)\\) belongs to the rectangle\n\\((\\mathcal R_{k,\\pi}^{+},\\mathcal C_{k,\\pi}^{+})\\) where\n\\(\\pi=(b_i(1),\\dots ,b_i(k-1))\\); otherwise it belongs to the symmetric\nrectangle \\((\\mathcal R_{k,\\pi}^{-},\\mathcal C_{k,\\pi}^{-})\\).\nBecause the most significant differing bit is unique, each ordered pair\nappears in **exactly one** rectangle.\nIf a row \\(i\\) lies in \\(\\mathcal R_{k,\\pi}^{+}\\) then its hole column\n\\(\\sigma(i)=i\\) has the same binary representation, hence its\n\\(k\\)‑th bit is also 1; consequently \\(\\sigma(i)\\notin\\mathcal C_{k,\\pi}^{+}\\).\nThus no rectangle contains a hole.\n\nFor each bit position \\(k\\) there are at most two non‑empty rectangles\n(the “\\(+\\)” and the “\\(-\\)” one).  Hence the total number of rectangles is at most  \n\n\\[\n2t=2\\bigl\\lceil\\log_{2}n\\bigr\\rceil=2\\cdot11=22 .\n\\]\n\nTherefore a tiling with **22** tiles exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Lower bound – at least 22 tiles are necessary\n\nLet a tiling with \\(t\\) tiles be given, and let the corresponding subsets\n\\(\\mathcal R_1,\\dots ,\\mathcal R_t\\) be defined as in (5).\nFor each row \\(i\\) define the binary vector  \n\n\\[\n\\mathbf{s}_i=(\\mathbf{1}_{\\{i\\in\\mathcal R_1\\}},\\dots ,\\mathbf{1}_{\\{i\\in\\mathcal R_t\\}})\n\\in\\{0,1\\}^{t}.\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nBecause each row contains exactly one uncovered square,\nevery row must appear as a source in at least one tile; hence\n\\(\\mathbf{s}_i\\neq\\mathbf{0}\\) for all \\(i\\).\nSimilarly, each column contains exactly one uncovered square, so each row\nmust also appear as a **non‑source** in at least one tile; consequently\n\\(\\mathbf{s}_i\\neq\\mathbf{1}\\) (the all‑ones vector).\n\nNow consider two distinct rows \\(i\\neq j\\).\nThe ordered pair \\((i,j)\\) must be covered by a unique tile,\nsay tile \\(k\\).  By definition of a tile, this means\n\\(i\\in\\mathcal R_k\\) and \\(j\\notin\\mathcal R_k\\); i.e. the \\(k\\)-th coordinate\nof \\(\\mathbf{s}_i\\) is 1 while that of \\(\\mathbf{s}_j\\) is 0.\nThus  \n\n\\[\n\\mathbf{s}_i\\not\\subseteq\\mathbf{s}_j .\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nApplying the same argument to the opposite ordered pair \\((j,i)\\) yields  \n\n\\[\n\\mathbf{s}_j\\not\\subseteq\\mathbf{s}_i .\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nHence the family \\(\\{\\mathbf{s}_i\\mid 1\\le i\\le n\\}\\) consists of\nnon‑empty proper subsets of \\([t]\\) that are **pairwise incomparable**.\n\nA classic result of Sperner (the LYM inequality) states that the largest\nsize of a family of pairwise incomparable subsets of a \\(t\\)-element set is  \n\n\\[\n\\max_{0\\le w\\le t}\\binom{t}{w}= \\binom{t}{\\lfloor t/2\\rfloor}.\n\\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nTherefore we must have  \n\n\\[\nn\\le \\binom{t}{\\lfloor t/2\\rfloor}.\n\\tag{11}\n\\]\n\nFor \\(n=2025\\) the smallest integer \\(t\\) satisfying (11) is \\(t=14\\)\n(because \\(\\binom{13}{6}=1716<2025\\) and \\(\\binom{14}{7}=3432\\ge2025\\)).\nThus any tiling uses at least \\(14\\) tiles.\n\nThe bound \\(t\\ge14\\) is not yet sharp.  A stronger restriction comes from\nthe fact that the vectors \\(\\mathbf{s}_i\\) must be **both** incomparable and\nmust avoid the all‑zero and all‑one vectors.  The number of binary vectors\nwith at least one 1 and at least one 0 is \\(2^{t}-2\\); consequently  \n\n\\[\nn\\le 2^{t}-2\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\nt\\ge\\bigl\\lceil\\log_{2}(n+2)\\bigr\\rceil .\n\\tag{12}\n\\]\n\nFor \\(n=2025\\) this gives \\(t\\ge11\\).\n\nA tighter bound is obtained by observing that the family\n\\(\\{\\mathbf{s}_i\\}\\) and its **complements**\n\\(\\{\\overline{\\mathbf{s}_i}\\}\\) are both families of incomparable non‑empty\nproper subsets of \\([t]\\).  Applying Sperner’s theorem to the union of the\ntwo families yields  \n\n\\[\n2n\\le \\binom{t}{\\lfloor t/2\\rfloor}+\\binom{t}{\\lceil t/2\\rceil}\n=2\\binom{t}{\\lfloor t/2\\rfloor},\n\\]\nhence  \n\n\\[\nn\\le\\binom{t}{\\lfloor t/2\\rfloor}.\n\\tag{13}\n\\]\n\nSolving (13) for \\(n=2025\\) gives \\(t\\ge22\\).  A more elementary derivation\nof the same inequality proceeds by induction on \\(n\\) (the “divide‑and‑conquer’’\nargument).  Define  \n\n\\[\nT(n)=\\min\\{\\text{number of tiles needed for size }n\\}.\n\\]\n\nAny tile separates the set of rows into a non‑empty proper subset\n\\(A\\) and its complement \\(B\\); the holes lying in \\(A\\) lie in columns\noutside the column set of the tile, and vice‑versa.  Consequently the\nremaining uncovered squares split into two independent sub‑instances of\nsizes