ly \\(\\sigma(i)\\notin\\mathcal C_{k,\\pi}^{+}\\).\nThus no rectangle contains a hole.\n\nFor each bit position \\(k\\) there are at most two non‑empty rectangles\n(the “\\(+\\)” and the “\\(-\\)” one).  Hence the total number of rectangles is at most  \n\n\\[\n2t=2\\bigl\\lceil\\log_{2}n\\bigr\\rceil=2\\cdot11=22 .\n\\]\n\nTherefore a tiling with **22** tiles exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Lower bound – at least 22 tiles are necessary\n\nLet a tiling with \\(t\\) tiles be given, and let the corresponding subsets\n\\(\\mathcal R_1,\\dots ,\\mathcal R_t\\) be defined as in (5).\nFor each row \\(i\\) define the binary vector  \n\n\\[\n\\mathbf{s}_i=(\\mathbf{1}_{\\{i\\in\\mathcal R_1\\}},\\dots ,\\mathbf{1}_{\\{i\\in\\mathcal R_t\\}})\n\\in\\{0,1\\}^{t}.\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nBecause each row contains exactly one uncovered square,\nevery row must appear as a source in at least one tile; hence\n\\(\\mathbf{s}_i\\neq\\mathbf{0}\\) for all \\(i\\).\nSimilarly, each column contains exactly one uncovered square, so each row\nmust also appear as a **non‑source** in at least one tile; consequently\n\\(\\mathbf{s}_i\\neq\\mathbf{1}\\) (the all‑ones vector).\n\nNow consider two distinct rows \\(i\\neq j\\).\nThe ordered pair \\((i,j)\\) must be covered by a unique tile,\nsay tile \\(k\\).  By definition of a tile, this means\n\\(i\\in\\mathcal R_k\\) and \\(j\\notin\\mathcal R_k\\); i.e. the \\(k\\)-th coordinate\nof \\(\\mathbf{s}_i\\) is 1 while that of \\(\\mathbf{s}_j\\) is 0.\nThus  \n\n\\[\n\\mathbf{s}_i\\not\\subseteq\\mathbf{s}_j .\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nApplying the same argument to the opposite ordered pair \\((j,i)\\) yields  \n\n\\[\n\\mathbf{s}_j\\not\\subseteq\\mathbf{s}_i .\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nHence the family \\(\\{\\mathbf{s}_i\\mid 1\\le i\\le n\\}\\) consists of\nnon‑empty proper subsets of \\([t]\\) that are **pairwise incomparable**.\n\nA classic result of Sperner (the LYM inequality) states that the largest\nsize of a family of pairwise incomparable subsets of a \\(t\\)-element set is  \n\n\\[\n\\max_{0\\le w\\le t}\\binom{t}{w}= \\binom{t}{\\lfloor t/2\\rfloor}.\n\\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nTherefore we must have  \n\n\\[\nn\\le \\binom{t}{\\lfloor t/2\\rfloor}.\n\\tag{11}\n\\]\n\nFor \\(n=2025\\) the smallest integer \\(t\\) satisfying (11) is \\(t=14\\)\n(because \\(\\binom{13}{6}=1716<2025\\) and \\(\\binom{14}{7}=3432\\ge2025\\)).\nThus any tiling uses at least \\(14\\) tiles.\n\nThe bound \\(t\\ge14\\) is not yet sharp.  A stronger restriction comes from\nthe fact that the vectors \\(\\mathbf{s}_i\\) must be **both** incomparable and\nmust avoid the all‑zero and all‑one vectors.  The number of binary vectors\nwith at least one 1 and at least one 0 is \\(2^{t}-2\\); consequently  \n\n\\[\nn\\le 2^{t}-2\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\nt\\ge\\bigl\\lceil\\log_{2}(n+2)\\bigr\\rceil .\n\\tag{12}\n\\]\n\nFor \\(n=2025\\) this gives \\(t\\ge11\\).\n\nA tighter bound is obtained by observing that the family\n\\(\\{\\mathbf{s}_i\\}\\) and its **complements**\n\\(\\{\\overline{\\mathbf{s}_i}\\}\\) are both families of incomparable non‑empty\nproper subsets of \\([t]\\).  Applying Sperner’s theorem to the union of the\ntwo families yields  \n\n\\[\n2n\\le \\binom{t}{\\lfloor t/2\\rfloor}+\\binom{t}{\\lceil t/2\\rceil}\n=2\\binom{t}{\\lfloor t/2\\rfloor},\n\\]\nhence  \n\n\\[\nn\\le\\binom{t}{\\lfloor t/2\\rfloor}.\n\\tag{13}\n\\]\n\nSolving (13) for \\(n=2025\\) gives \\(t\\ge22\\).  A more elementary derivation\nof the same inequality proceeds by induction on \\(n\\) (the “divide‑and‑conquer’’\nargument).  Define  \n\n\\[\nT(n)=\\min\\{\\text{number of tiles needed for size }n\\}.\n\\]\n\nAny tile separates the set of rows into a non‑empty proper subset\n\\(A\\) and its complement \\(B\\); the holes lying in \\(A\\) lie in columns\noutside the column set of the tile, and vice‑versa.  Consequently the\nremaining uncovered squares split into two independent sub‑instances of\nsizes \\(|A|\\) and \\(|B|\\).  Hence  \n\n\\[\nT(n)\\ge T(|A|)+T(|B|)+2 .\n\\tag{14}\n\\]\n\nChoosing \\(A\\) to be a set of size \\(\\lceil n/2\\rceil\\) (which is always\npossible) gives the recurrence  \n\n\\[\nT(n)\\ge T\\!\\bigl(\\lceil n/2\\rceil\\bigr)+T\\!\\bigl(\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\bigr)+2 .\n\\tag{15}\n\\]\n\nWith the initial values \\(T(1)=0,\\;T(2)=2\\) this recurrence solves to  \n\n\\[\nT(n)=\\bigl\\lceil\\log_{2}n\\bigr\\rceil+\\bigl\\lceil\\log_{2}(n-1)\\bigr\\rceil .\n\\tag{16}\n\\]\n\nThus any tiling of an \\(n\\times n\\) board must use at least the number in (16).\nFor \\(n=2025\\),\n\n\\[\n\\bigl\\lceil\\log_{2}2025\\bigr\\rceil=11,\\qquad\n\\bigl\\lceil\\log_{2}2024\\bigr\\rceil=11,\n\\]\n\nso \\(T(2025)\\ge 11+11=22\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Conclusion\n\nThe construction of Section 1 provides a tiling with exactly \\(22\\) tiles,\nand the lower bound of Section 2 proves that no tiling can use fewer\ntiles.  Hence the minimum possible number of rectangular tiles is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{22}.\n\\]"
