(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2\\).  \n   The tiles are pairwise disjoint and together cover every covered square.\n\n5. **Conclusion.**  \n   The lower bound and the explicit construction coincide; therefore the minimum possible number of tiles is \\(2n-2\\).  \n   For \\(n=2025\\) this equals \\(2\\cdot2025-2=4048\\).\n\n---\n\n**### Detailed Solution ###**  \n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nn=2025 .\n\\]\n\nBecause each row and each column must contain exactly one uncovered unit square, the set of uncovered squares is a *permutation matrix*.  \nHence there exists a permutation  \n\n\\[\np:\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\longrightarrow\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\n\\]\n\nsuch that the uncovered square in row \\(i\\) is the cell  \n\n\\[\n(i,p(i)) .\n\\]\n\nAll other cells are covered by tiles.  \n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  Splitting the covered region  \n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\nL=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le i,j\\le n,\\;j<p(i)\\},\n\\qquad \nR=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le i,j\\le n,\\;j>p(i)\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe sets \\(L\\) and \\(R\\) are disjoint and  \n\n\\[\n\\bigl([1,n]\\times[1,n]\\bigr)\\setminus\\{(i,p(i))\\}=L\\cup R .\n\\]\n\nThus a tiling of the whole board is the union of a tiling of \\(L\\) and a tiling of \\(R\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  A lower bound for the left region  \n\nFor each row \\(i\\) let  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_i:=p(i)-1 .\n\\]\n\nThe number \\(\\ell_i\\) is exactly the number of covered cells of row \\(i\\) that belong to \\(L\\).  \nBecause \\(p\\) is a permutation, the multiset \\(\\{\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_n\\}\\) equals  \n\n\\[\n\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-1\\}.\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nConsequently the *positive* lengths  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L:=\\{\\ell_i\\mid \\ell_i>0\\}\n\\]\n\nare the distinct numbers \\(1,2,\\dots ,n-1\\); in particular \\(|\\mathcal L|=n-1\\).\n\nConsider any rectangle \\(Q\\) used in a tiling of \\(L\\).  \nLet \\(I\\subseteq\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) be the set of rows covered by \\(Q\\) and let  \n\n\\[\nw:=\\max\\{j\\mid (i,j)\\in Q\\}\n\\]\n\nbe the rightmost column of \\(Q\\).  \nSince \\(Q\\subseteq L\\), for every \\(i\\in I\\) we must have \\(j\\le\\ell_i\\) for all cells \\((i,j)\\in Q\\); hence  \n\n\\[\nw\\le\\min_{i\\in I}\\ell_i .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nIf \\(\\min_{i\\in I}\\ell_i = m\\), then after placing \\(Q\\) all rows \\(i\\in I\\) with \\(\\ell_i=m\\) become completely covered (their left part is exhausted), whereas any row \\(i\\in I\\) with \\(\\ell_i>m\\) still retains uncovered cells in columns \\(m+1,\\dots ,\\ell_i\\).  \nThus a single rectangle can eliminate **at most one** element of the set \\(\\mathcal L\\).  \n\nStarting with \\(|\\mathcal L|=n-1\\) and decreasing by at most one per rectangle, any tiling of \\(L\\) needs at least \\(n-1\\) rectangles.  \n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  A lower bound for the right region  \n\nDefine for each row  \n\n\\[\nr_i:=n-p(i) .\n\\]\n\nExactly as in (1) the numbers \\(\\{r_1,\\dots ,r_n\\}\\) are the set \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\).  \nThe positive values \\(\\{r_i>0\\}\\) are again the distinct numbers \\(1,\\dots ,n-1\\).  \n\nRepeating the argument of §2 with the right region \\(R\\) (now the length of row \\(i\\) inside \\(R\\) equals \\(r_i\\)) shows that any tiling of \\(R\\) also requires at least \\(n-1\\) rectangles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Combined lower bound  \n\nSince a tiling of the whole board consists of a tiling of \\(L\\) together with a tiling of \\(R\\), we obtain\n\n\\[\n\\#\\text{tiles}\\;\\ge\\;(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2 .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  A tiling that uses exactly \\(2n-2\\) tiles  \n\nFor each row \\(i\\) we place at most two horizontal tiles:\n\n* **Left tile.**  \n  If \\(p(i)>1\\), place the rectangle  \n\n  \\[\n  H_i:=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le j\\le p(i)-1\\},\n  \\]\n\n  which is a \\(1\\times(p(i)-1)\\) tile covering the left part of row \\(i\\).\n\n* **Right tile.**  \n  If \\(p(i)<n\\), place the rectangle  \n\n  \\[\n  R_i:=\\{(i,j)\\mid p(i)+1\\le j\\le n\\},\n  \\]\n\n  a \\(1\\times\\bigl(n-p(i)\\bigr)\\) tile covering the right part of row \\(i\\).\n\nExactly one row satisfies \\(p(i)=1\\) (its left tile is empty) and exactly one row satisfies \\(p(i)=n\\) (its right tile is empty).  \nAll other rows contribute two non‑empty tiles.  \nHence the total number of non‑empty tiles is  \n\n\\[\n( n-1 )+( n-1 ) = 2n-2 .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nThe tiles are pairwise disjoint because each occupies a distinct set of cells in its own row, and together they cover every cell of \\(L\\cup R\\).  \nThus (4) is a valid tiling meeting the bound (3).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 6.  Conclusion  \n\nThe lower bound (3) and the explicit construction (4) coincide; therefore the minimum possible number of rectangular tiles is exactly \\(2n-2\\).  \nFor the given board with \\(n=2025\\),\n\n\\[\n2n-2 = 2\\cdot2025-2 = 4048 .\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{4048}\n\\]"
