e b\\) and \\(c\\le j\\le d\\).\n\nBecause no diagonal square may be covered, the rectangle must avoid all pairs\n\\((i,i)\\).  If the row interval and the column interval intersect, i.e. if there\nexists an integer \\(k\\) with \\(a\\le k\\le b\\) and \\(c\\le k\\le d\\), then the square\n\\((k,k)\\) would belong to the rectangle – a contradiction.  Hence we must have either  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{b<c}\\qquad\\text{(the rectangle lies entirely **above** the diagonal)},\n\\]\nor  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{d<a}\\qquad\\text{(the rectangle lies entirely **below** the diagonal)}.\n\\]\n\nThus every tile belongs to exactly one of the two triangular halves of the board,\nand the two halves can be tiled independently.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Lower bound for the upper half\n\nConsider the set of squares immediately above the diagonal  \n\n\\[\nS=\\{(i,i+1)\\mid i=1,2,\\dots ,n-1\\}.\n\\]\n\nLet \\(R\\) be a rectangle lying above the diagonal; write its row interval as\n\\([a,b]\\) and its column interval as \\([c,d]\\) with \\(b<c\\).  \nSuppose \\(R\\) contains two distinct squares of \\(S\\), say \\((i,i+1)\\) and\n\\((j,j+1)\\) with \\(i<j\\).  Then\n\n\\[\na\\le i\\le b,\\qquad c\\le i+1\\le d,\n\\]\n\\[\na\\le j\\le b,\\qquad c\\le j+1\\le d .\n\\]\n\nFrom \\(b<c\\le i+1\\) we obtain \\(i\\ge b\\); together with \\(i\\le b\\) this yields\n\\(i=b\\) and consequently \\(i+1=c\\).  The same reasoning applied to \\(j\\) gives\n\\(j=b\\) and \\(j+1=c\\).  Hence \\(i=j\\), contradicting the assumption that the two\nsquares are distinct.  Therefore\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{Every rectangle above the diagonal contains at most one element of }S.}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(|S|=n-1\\), covering all squares above the diagonal requires at least \\(n-1\\)\nrectangles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Lower bound for the lower half\n\nDefine the set of squares immediately below the diagonal  \n\n\\[\nT=