e same argument, any rectangle can contain at most one of the \\(B_i\\); thus at least another \\(n-1\\) rectangles are required for the right‑hand cells.\n\n   Since a rectangle cannot contain both a left‑hand cell \\(A_i\\) and a right‑hand cell \\(B_i\\) of the same row (it would have to cross the uncovered square), the total number of rectangles in any tiling is at least \\((n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2\\).\n\n4. **Conclusion:**  \n   The construction achieves the lower bound, so the minimal possible number of tiles is exactly \\(2n-2\\).  \n   For \\(n=2025\\) this equals \\(2\\cdot2025-2=4048\\).\n\n**### Detailed Solution ###**\n\nLet \\(n=2025\\).  \nBecause each row and each column must contain exactly one uncovered unit square, the set of uncovered squares is a permutation matrix.  \nDenote by \\(\\pi:\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\to\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) the permutation such that the uncovered square in row \\(i\\) is at column \\(\\pi(i)\\).  \nThus the uncovered squares are the points\n\\[\nU=\\{(i,\\pi(i))\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  An explicit tiling with \\(2n-2\\) tiles  \n\nFor each column \\(j\\) let \\(r_j=\\pi^{-1}(j)\\) be the unique row containing the uncovered square in that column.  \n\n*Above‑tile.* If \\(r_j>1\\), place the vertical rectangle\n\\[\nT_j^{\\text{up}}=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le i< r_j\\}.\n\\]\n\n*Below‑tile.* If \\(r_j<n\\), place the vertical rectangle\n\\[\nT_j^{\\text{down}}=\\{(i,j)\\mid r_j< i\\le n\\}.\n\\]\n\nEach \\(T_j^{\\text{up}}\\) and \\(T_j^{\\text{down}}\\) is a rectangle whose sides lie on grid lines, the rectangles are pairwise disjoint, and together they cover every unit square that is not in \\(U\\).  \n\nThe column whose uncovered square lies in the top row (\\(r_j=1\\)) contributes only a below‑tile; the column whose uncovered square lies in the bottom row (\\(r_j=n\\)) contributes only an above‑tile.  \nAll other columns contribute both an above‑tile and a below‑tile.  \nHence the total number of tiles is\n\\[\n(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  A lower bound of \\(2n-2\\) tiles  \n\nConsider the cells that lie immediately to the left of the uncovered squares.  \nFor each row \\(i\\) with \\(\\pi(i)>1\\) define\n\\[\nA_i=(i,\\pi(i)-1).\n\\]\nThe columns \\(\\pi(i)-1\\) are all distinct, so the set \\(\\{A_i\\mid \\pi(i)>1\\}\\) contains exactly \\(n-1\\) squares.\n\n**Claim.** No rectangle in a valid tiling can contain two distinct squares \\(A_i\\) and \\(A_k\\).\n\n*Proof of claim.* Assume a rectangle \\(R\\) contains \\(A_i\\) and \\(A_k\\) with \\(i\\neq k\\).  \nWithout loss of generality \\(\\pi(i)\\le \\pi(k)\\); then the column interval of \\(R\\) contains both columns \\(\\pi(i)-1\\) and \\(\\pi(k)-1\\), and therefore also column \\(\\pi(i)\\).  \nSince \\(R\\) also contains row \\(i\\) (it contains \\(A_i\\)), it would contain the uncovered square \\((i,\\pi(i))\\), contradicting the requirement that uncovered squares are not covered. ∎\n\nConsequently each rectangle can cover **at most one** of the squares \\(A_i\\).  \nAll \\(n-1\\) squares \\(A_i\\) must be covered, so at least \\(n-1\\) rectangles are necessary to cover the part of the board that lies left of the holes.\n\nA symmetric argument applied to the cells immediately to the right of the holes yields another set of \\(n-1\\) distinct squares\n\\[\nB_i=(i,\\pi(i)+1)\\qquad(\\pi(i)<n),\n\\]\nwith the property that any rectangle can contain at most one of them.  \nThus at least another \\(n-1\\) rectangles are needed for the right‑hand part of the board.\n\nA rectangle cannot simultaneously contain a left‑hand cell \\(A_i\\) and a right‑hand cell \\(B_i\\) of the same row, because that would force the rectangle to cross the uncovered square \\((i,\\pi(i))\\).  \nHence the total number of rectangles in any admissible tiling is at least\n\\[\n(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Minimality  \n\nThe construction of §1 uses exactly \\(2n-2\\) rectangles, and §2 shows that no tiling can use fewer.  \nTherefore the minimum possible number of tiles is \\(2n-2\\).\n\nFor the given board \\(n=2025\\),\n\\[\n2n-2 = 2\\cdot2025-2 = 4050-2 = 4048.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Answer:** \\(\\boxed{4048}\\)."
