{k}\\) is an **inner corner** of \\(A\\): it is present,\nits right neighbour \\((k,k+2)\\) is present, its lower neighbour\n\\((k+1,k+1)\\) is missing.\n\nAnalogously, column \\(j\\) of \\(B\\) contains \\(n-j\\) squares, so the cells  \n\n\\[\nd_{k}=(k+1,k)\\qquad(k=1,\\dots ,n-1)\n\\]\n\nare the inner corners of \\(B\\).\n\nHence both \\(A\\) and \\(B\\) possess exactly \\(n-1\\) inner corners.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  At most one inner corner per rectangle  \n\n\\[\n\\textbf{Lemma 2.}\\qquad\n\\text{A rectangle contained in }A\\text{ (resp. }B\\text{) contains at most\none inner corner of }A\\text{ (resp. }B\\text{).}\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nSuppose a rectangle \\(R\\subseteq A\\) contained two inner corners\n\\(c_{i}=(i,i+1)\\) and \\(c_{j}=(j,j+1)\\) with \\(i<j\\).\nBecause \\(R\\) is a Cartesian product of a row interval and a column\ninterval, it also contains the whole sub\u2011rectangle\n\\([i,i+1]\\times[i+1,j+1]\\), and in particular the cell\n\\((i+1,i+1)\\).  This cell is a hole, contradicting \\(R\\subseteq A\\).\nThe same argument works for \\(B\\). \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 6.  Lower bound for the number of tiles  \n\nFrom Lemma\u202f2 each rectangle can cover at most one inner corner, while\nLemma\u202f4 shows that every inner corner must be covered.  \nTherefore any tiling of \\(A\\) uses at least \\(n-1\\) rectangles, and any\ntiling of \\(B\\) uses at least \\(n-1\\) rectangles.  Consequently\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{any admissible tiling uses at least }2n-2\\text{ tiles}.}\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 7.  A tiling with exactly \\(2n-2\\) tiles  \n\nChoose the permutation \\(\\pi(i)=i\\); the uncovered squares are the\n\\(n\\) diagonal cells \\((i,i)\\).\n\n*Tiles covering \\(A\\) (the cells above the diagonal).*  \nFor each \\(k=1,\\dots ,n-1\\) let  \n\n\\[\nH_{k}= \\{(k,j)\\mid j=k+1,\\dots ,n\\},\n\\]\n\na \\(1\\times (n-k)\\) rectangle lying in row \\(k\\) to the right of the hole\n\\((k,k)\\).  \nThe family \\(\\{H_{1},\\dots ,H_{n-1}\\}\\) tiles the whole region \\(A\\).\n\n*Tiles covering \\(B\\) (the cells below the diagonal).*  \nFor each \\(k=1,\\dots ,n-1\\) let  \n\n\\[\nV_{k}= \\{(i,k)\\mid i=k+1,\\dots ,n\\},\n\\]\n\nan \\((n-k)\\times1\\) rectangle lying in column \\(k\\) below the hole\n\\((k,k)\\).  \nThe family \\(\\{V_{1},\\dots ,V_{n-1}\\}\\) tiles the whole region \\(B\\).\n\nAll \\(2n-2\\) rectangles are pairwise disjoint, each lies entirely in\n\\(A\\) or \\(B\\), and together they cover every square except the diagonal\nholes.  Hence a tiling with exactly \\(2n-2\\) tiles exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 8.  Minimality  \n\nEquation\u202f(1) gives a universal lower bound of \\(2n-2\\) tiles, and the\nconstruction of \u00a7\u202f7 shows that this bound is attainable.  Therefore the\nminimum possible number of tiles equals \\(2n-2\\).\n\nFor the concrete board with \\(n=2025\\),\n\n\\[\n2n-2 = 2\\cdot2025-2 = 4050-2 = 4048 .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 9.  Answer  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{4048}\n\\]"
