\\},\\qquad k=1,\\dots ,n .\n\\]\n\nHence \\(A\\) is a **Young diagram** (Ferrers shape) with \\(\\lambda_k\\) cells in its\n\\(k\\)-th row (counted from the top) and with \\(n-1\\) non\u2011empty rows.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Lemma: a Young diagram with \\(m\\) non\u2011empty rows needs at least \\(m\\) rectangles  \n\n*Proof.*  \nIn a left\u2011justified Young diagram the rightmost cell of each non\u2011empty row is a\n**corner** of the diagram.  \nSuppose a rectangle \\(R\\) (with sides on grid lines) contains two distinct corners,\nsay those belonging to rows \\(r<s\\).  \nBecause the diagram is left\u2011justified, the column of the corner in row \\(s\\) is\n\\(\\le\\) the column of the corner in row \\(r\\).  \nConsequently \\(R\\) would also contain the cell \\((r,\\text{column of corner in row }s)\\),\nwhich lies **outside** the diagram (the diagram ends earlier in row \\(r\\)).  \nThus a rectangle can contain at most one corner.  \nSince there are exactly \\(m\\) corners, at least \\(m\\) rectangles are required. \u220e\n\nApplying the lemma to the diagram \\(A\\) (which has \\(n-1\\) non\u2011empty rows) yields\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{any tiling of }A\\text{ uses at least }n-1\\text{ rectangles}.}\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nExactly the same reasoning applied to the set  \n\n\\[\nB=\\{(i,j)\\mid j<\\pi(i)\\}\n\\]\n\n(after ordering the rows by decreasing \\(\\pi(i)\\)) shows that any tiling of \\(B\\)\nneeds at least \\(n-1\\) rectangles as well:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{any tiling of }B\\text{ uses at least }n-1\\text{ rectangles}.}\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nSince the two regions are disjoint, any tiling of the whole board must contain at\nleast the sum of the two lower bounds:\n\n\\[\n\\text{number of tiles}\\;\\ge\\;(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2 .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  A tiling with exactly \\(2n-2\\) tiles  \n\nTake the identity permutation \\(\\pi(i)=i\\