joint tiling of \\(A\\) and of \\(B\\).\n\n---\n\n#### 1.  Lower bound for \\(A\\)\n\nReorder the rows so that \\(\\pi\\) is increasing: there exists a permutation \\(\\sigma\\) of \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) such that\n\n\\[\n\\pi(\\sigma(1))<\\pi(\\sigma(2))<\\dots <\\pi(\\sigma(n)).\n\\]\n\nFor each \\(k\\) set\n\n\\[\n\\lambda_k=n-\\pi(\\sigma(k)).\n\\]\n\nBecause the \\(\\pi(\\sigma(k))\\) are strictly increasing, the sequence \\(\\lambda_1\\ge\\lambda_2\\ge\\dots\\ge\\lambda_{n-1}>0\\) and \\(\\lambda_n=0\\).  \nAfter applying the row permutation \\(\\sigma\\), the set \\(A\\) becomes\n\n\\[\nA=\\bigcup_{k=1}^{n}\\{(\\sigma(k),j)\\mid 1\\le j\\le\\lambda_k\\},\n\\]\n\ni.e. a left‑justified Young diagram with row lengths \\(\\lambda_k\\).  \nThus \\(A\\) has exactly \\(n-1\\) non‑empty rows (the row corresponding to \\(\\pi=n\\) is empty).\n\n**Lemma.** *Let \\(Y\\) be a left‑justified Young diagram with \\(m\\) non‑empty rows. Then any tiling of \\(Y\\) by axis‑parallel rectangles uses at least \\(m\\) rectangles.*\n\n*Proof.* In each non‑empty row of \\(Y\\) the rightmost cell is a **corner** of the diagram. Suppose a rectangle \\(R\\) contained in \\(Y\\) contains two distinct corners, belonging to rows \\(r<s\\). Let the row lengths be \\(\\ell_r\\ge\\ell_s\\). The rectangle must contain the cell \\((s,\\ell_r)\\) because its column interval includes the column \\(\\ell_r\\) (the column of the corner in row \\(r\\)). But row \\(s\\) contains cells only up to column \\(\\ell_s<\\ell_r\\); hence \\((s,\\ell_r)\\) lies outside \\(Y\\), contradicting that \\(R\\subseteq Y\\). Therefore each rectangle can contain at most one corner. Since there are \\(m\\) corners, at least \\(m\\) rectangles are required. ∎\n\nApplying the lemma to the Young diagram representing \\(A\\) (with \\(m=n-1\\)) gives\n\n\\[\n\\text{any tiling of }A\\text{ uses at least }n-1\\text{ rectangles}. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n#### 2.  Lower bound for \\(B\\)\n\nAnalogously, reorder the rows so that \\(\\pi\\) is decreasing: there exists a permutation \\(\\tau\\) such that\n\n\\[\n\\pi(\\tau(1))>\\pi(\\tau(2))>\\dots >\\pi(\\tau(n)).\n\\]\n\nDefine \\(\\mu_k=\\pi(\\tau(k))-1\\). Then \\(\\mu_1>\\mu_2>\\dots>\\mu_{n-1}>0\\) and \\(\\mu_n=0\\).  \nAfter applying \\(\\tau\\), the set \\(B\\) becomes a left‑justified Young diagram with row lengths \\(\\mu_k\\); it also has exactly \\(n-1\\) non‑empty rows. By the same lemma,\n\n\\[\n\\text{any tiling of }B\\text{ uses at least }n-1\\text{ rectangles}. \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n#### 3.  Global lower bound\n\nSince the tiling of the whole board consists of a tiling of \\(A\\) together with a tiling of \\(B\\) and the two parts are disjoint, (1) and (2) imply\n\n\\[\n\\#\\text{tiles}\\;\\ge\\;(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2. \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n#### 4.  Construction achieving the bound\n\nTake any permutation \\(\\pi\\) (for concreteness the identity \\(\\pi(i)=i\\)).  \nFor each row \\(i\\) place\n\n* a **left tile** covering the cells \\((i,1),(i,2),\\dots,(i,\\pi(i)-1)\\) if \\(\\pi(i)>1\\);\n* a **right tile** covering the cells \\((i,\\pi(i)+1),\\dots,(i,n)\\) if \\(\\pi(i)<n\\).\n\nEach tile is a rectangle of size \\(1\\times(\\pi(i)-1)\\) or \\(1\\times(n-\\pi(i))\\); tiles lie in distinct rows, hence they do not overlap, and none contains the uncovered cell \\((i,\\pi(i))\\).  \n\nThe total number of tiles equals\n\n\\[\n\\sum_{i=1}^{n}\\bigl(\\mathbf 1_{\\{\\pi(i)>1\\}}+\\mathbf 1_{\\{\\pi(i)<n\\}}\\bigr)\n=(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2,\n\\]\n\nbecause exactly one row has \\(\\pi(i)=1\\) and exactly one row has \\(\\pi(i)=n\\).\n\nThus a tiling with \\(2n-2\\) tiles exists.\n\n---\n\n#### 5.  Minimality and final answer\n\nInequality (3) shows that no tiling can use fewer than \\(2n-2\\) tiles, while the construction above achieves exactly \\(2n-2\\) tiles. Hence the minimum possible number of tiles is \\(2n-2\\).\n\nFor \\(n=2025\\),\n\n\\[\n2n-2 = 2\\cdot2025-2 = 4050-2 = \\boxed{4048}.\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### Self-Correction Instruc