iles are pairwise interior‑disjoint,\n* every row and every column contains **exactly one** uncovered unit square.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  The uncovered squares form a permutation matrix  \n\nBecause each row contains precisely one uncovered square, there is a function  \n\\[\n\\pi:\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\longrightarrow\\{1,\\dots ,n\\},\\qquad \n\\text{where }\\pi(i)=\\text{column of the uncovered square in row }i .\n\\]\nSimilarly, each column contains exactly one uncovered square, so \\(\\pi\\) is a bijection; i.e. a permutation of \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).  \nThus the set of uncovered squares is\n\\[\nU=\\{(i,\\pi(i))\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  An upper bound of \\(2n-2\\)\n\nFor a fixed row \\(i\\) define  \n\n\\[\nL_i=\\{(i,j):j<\\pi(i)\\},\\qquad \nR_i=\\{(i,j):j>\\pi(i)\\}.\n\\]\n\n\\(L_i\\) (resp. \\(R_i\\)) is the **left** (resp. **right**) part of row \\(i\\) that must be covered.  \nIf \\(\\pi(i)=1\\) then \\(L_i=\\varnothing\\); if \\(\\pi(i)=n\\) then \\(R_i=\\varnothing\\).\n\nBecause \\(\\pi\\) is a permutation, exactly one row has \\(\\pi(i)=1\\) and exactly one row has \\(\\pi(i)=n\\).  \nHence the number of non‑empty left parts is \\(n-1\\) and the number of non‑empty right parts is also \\(n-1\\).\n\nPlace a separate \\(1\\times |L_i|\\) tile on each non‑empty \\(L_i\\) and a separate \\(1\\times |R_i|\\) tile on each non‑empty \\(R_i\\).  \nThese tiles are disjoint, cover all squares outside \\(U\\), and their total number is  \n\n\\[\n(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2 .\n\\]\n\nThus  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{f(n)\\le 2n-2}.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  A lower bound of \\(2n-2\\)\n\nWe treat the left and right sides separately.\n\n##### 3.1  Left side\n\nConsider the set  \n\n\\[\nL=\\bigcup_{i=1}^{n}L_i=\\{(i,j):j<\\pi(i)\\}.\n\\]\n\nFor each row with \\(\\pi(i)>1\\) define the **right‑most covered cell**\n\\[\na_i=(i,\\pi(i)-1).\n\\]\nThere are exactly \\(n-1\\) such cells because precisely one row satisfies \\(\\pi(i)=1\\).\n\n*Lemma 1.*  \nIf a rectangle \\(R\\) (with sides on grid lines) contains a cell \\(a_i\\), then the rightmost column of \\(R\\) is \\(\\pi(i)-1\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nSince \\(R\\) is a rectangle, its column interval is a contiguous set \\([c_1,c_2]\\).  \nBecause \\(a_i\\) lies in column \\(\\pi(i)-1\\), we have \\(c_2\\ge\\pi(i)-1\\).  \nIf \\(c_2>\\pi(i)-1\\), then column \\(\\pi(i)\\) belongs to \\([c_1,c_2]\\); but \\((i,\\pi(i))\\) is uncovered, so \\(R\\) would contain a hole – impossible.  \nThus \\(c_2=\\pi(i)-1\\). ∎\n\nConsequently, any rectangle covering two distinct cells \\(a_i\\) and \\(a_{i'}\\) would have to end simultaneously at columns \\(\\pi(i)-1\\) and \\(\\pi(i')-1\\).  \nSince the values \\(\\pi(i)-1\\) are all distinct (the \\(\\pi(i)\\) are a permutation), this is impossible.  \nHence **each rectangle can contain at most one cell \\(a_i\\).**\n\nBecause the \\(n-1\\) cells \\(a_i\\) must all be covered, at least \\(n-1\\) rectangles are required to cover the whole set \\(L\\).\n\n##### 3.2  Right side\n\nDefine the right side  \n\n\\[\nR=\\bigcup_{i=1}^{n}R_i=\\{(i,j):j>\\pi(i)\\}.\n\\]\n\nFor each row with \\(\\pi(i)<n\\) let  \n\n\\[\nb_i=(i,\\pi(i)+1)\n\\]\n(the left‑most covered cell of that row).  \nAgain there are exactly \\(n-1\\) such cells, because exactly one row has \\(\\pi(i)=n\\).\n\n*Lemma 2.*  \nIf a rectangle contains a cell \\(b_i\\), then its leftmost column is \\(\\pi(i)+1\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nThe column interval of the rectangle is \\([c_1,c_2]\\) with \\(c_1\\le\\pi(i)+1\\le c_2\\).  \nIf \\(c_1<\\pi(i)+1\\), then column \\(\\pi(i)\\) lies in the interval, and \\((i,\\pi(i))\\) (a hole) would be inside the rectangle – contradiction.  \nThus \\(c_1=\\pi(i)+1\\). ∎\n\nExactly as before, distinct rows give distinct values \\(\\pi(i)+1\\); therefore a rectangle can contain at most one cell \\(b_i\\).  \nHence at least \\(n-1\\) rectangles are needed to cover the right side \\(R\\).\n\n##### 3.3  Disjointness of the two families\n\nSuppose a rectangle \\(Q\\) covered a left‑hand cell \\(a_i\\) and a right‑hand cell \\(b_j\\).  \nFrom Lemma 1 the rightmost column of \\(Q\\) is \\(\\pi(i)-1\\); from Lemma 2 the leftmost column of \\(Q\\) is \\(\\pi(j)+1\\).  \nIf \\(i=j\\) then \\(\\pi(i)-1<\\pi(i)<\\pi(i)+1\\), so the interval would contain column \\(\\pi(i)\\), a hole – impossible.  \nIf \\(i\\neq j\\), the column interval would contain both \\(\\pi(i)\\) and \\(\\pi(j)\\), again a hole in the corresponding row.  \nThus no rectangle can cover simultaneously a cell of type \\(a_i\\) and a cell of type \\(b_j\\).  \nConsequently the rectangles needed for the left side and those needed for the right side are disjoint sets.\n\nCombining the two lower bounds we obtain  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{f(n)\\ge (n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2 } .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Exact value\n\nFrom Sections 2 and 3 we have both  \n\n\\[\n2n-2\\le f(n)\\le 2n-2,\n\\]\n\nhence  \n\n\\[\nf(n)=2n-2 .\n\\]\n\nFor the concrete board with \\(n=2025\\),\n\n\\[\nf(2025)=2\\cdot2025-2=4050-2=4048 .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  Answer\n\nMatilda can achieve the required condition with **4048** rectangular tiles, and no arrangement can use fewer.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{4048}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### Self-Correction Instruction ###\n\nReview the “Summary” and “Detailed Solution” sections to ensure:\n\n* All mathematical statements are expressed in TeX.\n* The permutation model, upper‑bound construction, and the two lemmas are presented with complete, rigorous proofs.\n* No step relies on intuition without justification.\n* The final answer is correctly formatted as \\(\\boxed{4048}\\)."
