  Choose the permutation \\(p(i)=i\\) (the main diagonal).  \n   - For each \\(k=1,2,\\dots ,n-1\\) place a rectangle covering rows \\(1,\\dots ,k\\) and columns \\(k+1,\\dots ,n\\).  \n     These \\(n-1\\) rectangles tile all squares **above** the diagonal.  \n   - For each \\(k=1,2,\\dots ,n-1\\) place a rectangle covering rows \\(k+1,\\dots ,n\\) and columns \\(1,\\dots ,k\\).  \n     These \\(n-1\\) rectangles tile all squares **below** the diagonal.  \n\n   The \\(n\\) diagonal squares remain uncovered, providing exactly one uncovered square in each row and each column.  \n   The two families of rectangles are pairwise disjoint and together cover every non‑diagonal square, so the total number of tiles is \\(2n-2\\).  \n\n5. **Conclusion.**  \n   The lower bound \\(2n-2\\) and the explicit construction both give the same value; therefore the minimum possible number of tiles is \\(2n-2\\). For \\(n=2025\\),\n\n\\[\n2\\cdot2025-2=4048.\n\\]\n\n---  \n\n**### Detailed Solution ###**  \n\nLet the grid consist of the unit squares \\((i,j)\\) with \\(1\\le i,j\\le n\\) where \\(n=2025\\).  \nA *tile* is a rectangle whose sides lie on grid lines; different tiles may not overlap and each unit square may belong to at most one tile.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  The uncovered squares form a permutation matrix  \n\nBecause each row must contain exactly one uncovered square, there are at least \\(n\\) uncovered squares.  \nSince each column must also contain exactly one uncovered square, the set of uncovered squares contains exactly one square in each column.  \nHence the uncovered squares are precisely the squares  \n\n\\[\nU=\\{(i,p(i))\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n\\},\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(p:\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\to\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) is a bijection (a permutation).  \nFor the rest of the proof we fix such a permutation \\(p\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Left‑hand and right‑hand regions  \n\nFor each row \\(i\\) define  \n\n\\[\nL_i:=p(i)-1\\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad R_i:=n-p(i).\n\\]\n\nThe squares **left** of the uncovered square in row \\(i\\) are  \n\n\\[\n\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le j\\le L_i\\},\n\\]\n\nand the squares **right** of the uncovered square in row \\(i\\) are  \n\n\\[\n\\{(i,j)\\mid p(i)+1\\le j\\le n\\}=\\{(i,j)\\mid L_i+2\\le j\\le n\\}.\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(p\\) is a bijection, the numbers \\(L_i\\) (and also the numbers \\(R_i\\)) are all distinct and satisfy  \n\n\\[\n\\{L_i\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n\\}=\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\},\n\\qquad\n\\{R_i\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n\\}=\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\}.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  A lower bound for the left‑hand side  \n\nConsider any tile \\(T\\) that lies completely to the left of all holes; thus every square of \\(T\\) belongs to the set \\(\\{(i,j)\\mid j\\le L_i\\}\\).  \nLet the column interval of \\(T\\) be \\([1,c]\\) (the left side of a tile is always column 1 because the tile cannot cross a hole).  \nSince \\(T\\) cannot contain a hole, we must have  \n\n\\[\nc\\le \\min_{i\\in\\mathcal R(T)} L_i,\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(\\mathcal R(T)\\) denotes the set of rows occupied by \\(T\\).\n\nNow look at the **rightmost** square of each row that belongs to the left‑hand region, namely \\((i,L_i)\\) (if \\(L_i>0\\)).  \nSuppose that a tile \\(T\\) contains two such squares \\((i,L_i)\\) and \\((j,L_j)\\) with \\(i\\neq j\\).  \nWithout loss of generality \\(L_i<L_j\\).  \nBecause \\((j,L_j)\\in T\\), the rightmost column of \\(T\\) is at least \\(L_j\\); consequently column \\(L_i+1\\) also belongs to \\(T\\).  \nBut column \\(L_i+1\\) in row \\(i\\) is precisely the uncovered square \\((i,p(i))\\), which a tile may not cover.  \nThis contradiction shows:\n\n> **Lemma 1.** A tile situated entirely to the left of all holes can contain **at most one** square of the form \\((i,L_i)\\) with \\(L_i>0\\).\n\nSince the values \\(L_i\\) are distinct, there are exactly \\(n-1\\) rows with \\(L_i>0\\).  \nBy Lemma 1 each such square requires a different tile, so at least \\(n-1\\) tiles are necessary to cover the whole left‑hand region.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  A lower bound for the right‑hand side  \n\nDefine the **leftmost** square of the right‑hand region in row \\(i\\) as \\((i,p(i)+1)\\) (present only when \\(R_i>0\\)).  \nThe same argument as above, applied to rectangles lying entirely to the right of all holes, yields:\n\n> **Lemma 2.** A tile situated entirely to the right of all holes can contain at most one square of the form \\((i,p(i)+1)\\) with \\(R_i>0\\).\n\nBecause the numbers \\(R_i=n-p(i)\\) are also the distinct set \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\), there are again exactly \\(n-1\\) rows with \\(R_i>0\\).  \nConsequently at least \\(n-1\\) tiles are required for the right‑hand region.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  Global lower bound  \n\nCombining the two sides we obtain\n\n\\[\n\\text{(total number of tiles)}\\;\\ge\\;(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2.\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 6.  Construction attaining the bound  \n\nTake the permutation \\(p(i)=i\\); i.e., the uncovered squares are the diagonal squares \\((i,i)\\) for \\(i=1,\\dots ,n\\).\n\n*Above the diagonal.*  \nFor each integer \\(k\\) with \\(1\\le k\\le n-1\\) place the rectangle  \n\n\\[\nT_k^{\\text{up}}=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le i\\le k,\\;k+1\\le j\\le n\\}.\n\\]\n\n\\(T_k^{\\text{up}}\\) has size \\(k\\times (n-k)\\) and lies entirely above the diagonal, hence contains no uncovered square.  \nThe family \\(\\{T_k^{\\text{up}}\\}_{k=1}^{n-1}\\) is pairwise disjoint and together they cover **all** squares with \\(i<j\\) (the whole region above the diagonal).\n\n*Below the diagonal.*  \nFor each integer \\(k\\) with \\(1\\le k\\le n-1\\) place the rectangle  \n\n\\[\nT_k^{\\text{down}}=\\{(i,j)\\mid k+1\\le i\\le n,\\;1\\le j\\le k\\}.\n\\]\n\n\\(T_k^{\\text{down}}\\) has size \\((n-k)\\times k\\) and lies entirely below the diagonal.  \nThe family \\(\\{T_k^{\\text{down}}\\}_{k=1}^{n-1}\\) is pairwise disjoint and together they cover **all** squares with \\(i>j\\) (the whole region below the diagonal).\n\nThe diagonal squares \\((i,i)\\) remain uncovered, providing exactly one uncovered square in each row and each column.  \nThe total number of tiles used is  \n\n\\[\n(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2.\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 7.  Optimality  \n\nInequality (1) shows that no admissible tiling can use fewer than \\(2n-2\\) tiles.  \nConstruction (2) shows that \\(2n-2\\) tiles are sufficient.  \nHence the minimum possible number of tiles equals \\(2n-2\\).\n\nFor the given board \\(n=2025\\),\n\n\\[\n2\\cdot2025-2 = 4048.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Answer:** \\(\\boxed{4048}\\)."
