gion\". Since there are n-1 such squares on each side, we need at least n-1 tiles for left side and n-1 for right side, total at least 2n-2. The construction uses exactly that many.\n\nWe need to check if the lower bound is valid. The lemmas claim that any tile that lies entirely in left-hand region can contain at most one of the squares (i, L_i) where L_i>0. Similarly for right-hand region. Since there are n-1 such squares, we need at least n-1 tiles covering left-hand region. However, is it possible that a tile covering left-hand region also covers some squares in right-hand region? No, because a tile cannot cross the uncovered square in its row; but a tile could possibly cover squares from both left and right sides of different rows? Let's examine: The left-hand region consists of squares left of the uncovered square in each row. The right-hand region consists of squares right of the uncovered square in each row. Since each row has exactly one uncovered square, a tile cannot cross that uncovered square within a row because it would cover that square. However, a tile could be placed such that it covers squares from left side of some rows and right side of other rows, but not crossing the uncovered square in any row. But the lemmas consider tiles that lie entirely in left-hand region (i.e., for each square (i,j) in T, j ≤ L_i). That means the tile's columns are limited to the left side of each row's uncovered square. For a tile to be in left-hand region, it must be wholly left of the uncovered squares in all rows it occupies. Similarly for right-hand region.\n\nThus the lemmas are correct: any tile wholly in left-hand region can contain at most one of the squares (i, L_i). Because if it contains two such squares (i, L_i) and (j, L_j) with L_i < L_j, then the tile must include column L_i+1 for row i, which is the uncovered square (i, p(i)). So contradiction. So each tile can cover at most one of those squares. So we need at least n-1 tiles covering left-hand region.