"### Summary ###\n\n**a. Verdict:**  \nI have successfully solved the problem. The minimum number of tiles required is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{4048}.\n\\]\n\n**b. Method Sketch:**  \n1. **Structure of uncovered squares.**  \n   The condition \u201ceach row and each column has exactly one uncovered unit square\u2019\u2019 forces the uncovered squares to form a permutation matrix: there is a bijection \\(p:\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\to\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) such that the uncovered square in row \\(i\\) is \\((i,p(i))\\).  \n   By permuting rows and columns simultaneously we may assume \\(p(i)=i\\) for all \\(i\\); i.e. the uncovered squares lie on the main diagonal.  \n   Let \\(n=2025\\).\n\n2. **Lower bound (at least \\(2n-2\\) tiles).**  \n   - Consider the set of cells just to the right of the diagonal  \n\n     \\[\n     S=\\{(i,i+1)\\mid 1\\le i\\le n-1\\}.\n     \\]\n\n     The cells of \\(S\\) lie in distinct rows and distinct columns.  \n   - Any axis\u2011aligned rectangle that contains two distinct cells of \\(S\\) must also contain a diagonal cell \\((j,j)\\) (the uncovered square), which is forbidden.  \n     Hence each rectangle can contain **at most one** cell of \\(S\\).  \n   - Since the region above the diagonal consists of all cells \\((i,j)\\) with \\(i<j\\), every cell of \\(S\\) must be covered, so at least \\(|S|=n-1\\) rectangles are needed for the upper region.  \n   - By symmetry, the set  \n\n     \\[\n     T=\\{(i+1,i)\\mid 1\\le i\\le n-1\\}\n     \\]\n\n     lies just below the diagonal, and the same argument shows that at least \\(n-1\\) rectangles are needed for the lower region.  \n   - Consequently any tiling of the whole board requires at least  \n\n     \\[\n     (n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2\n     \\]\n\n     tiles.\n\n3. **Construction achieving the bound (exactly \\(2n-2\\) tiles).**  \n   - For each row \\(i\\) with \\(1\\le i\\le n-1\\) place a tile  \n\n     \\[\n     R_i=\\{(i,j)\\mid i+1\\le j\\le n\\},\n     \\]\n\n     i.e. the contiguous block of squares to the right of the uncovered square in that row.  \n   - For each row \\(i\\) with \\(2\\le i\\le n\\) place a tile  \n\n     \\[\n     L_i=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le j\\le i-1\\},\n     \\]\n\n     i.e. the contiguous block of squares to the left of the uncovered square in that row.  \n   - The tiles \\(R_i\\) (resp. \\(L_i\\)) are pairwise disjoint because they lie in distinct rows, and none of them contains a diagonal cell.  \n   - The collection \\(\\{R_i\\}_{i=1}^{n-1}\\cup\\{L_i\\}_{i=2}^{n}\\) covers every unit square except the \\(n\\) diagonal squares, using exactly  \n\n     \\[\n     (n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2\n     \\]\n\n     tiles.\n\n4. **Conclusion.**  \n   The lower bound \\(2n-2\\) and the explicit construction both give the same number, so the minimum possible number of tiles is \\(2n-2\\). For \\(n=2025\\) this equals \\(4048\\).\n\n---\n\n### Detailed Solution ###\n\nLet the board consist of the unit squares \\((i,j)\\) with \\(1\\le i,j\\le n\\), where \\(n=2025\\).  \nA **tile** is a rectangle whose sides lie on grid lines; it therefore consists of a set  \n\n\\[\n\\{(i,j)\\mid a\\le i\\le b,\\;c\\le j\\le d\\}\n\\]\n\nfor some integers \\(1\\le a\\le b\\le n\\) and \\(1\\le c\\le d\\le n\\).  \nTiles are required to be pairwise interior\u2011disjoint (no unit square is covered by more than one tile).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  Form of the uncovered squares\n\nBecause each row and each column contains exactly one uncovered square, there exists a bijection  \n\n\\[\np:\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\longrightarrow\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\n\\]\n\nsuch that the uncovered square in row \\(i\\) is \\((i,p(i))\\).  \nApplying the same permutation to the rows and the inverse permutation to the columns sends the set \\(\\{(i,p(i))\\}\\) to the main diagonal \\(\\{(i,i)\\}\\) and transforms any tiling into a tiling of the same size.  \nHence we may (and shall) assume\n\n\\[\n\\text{the uncovered squares are }(i,i),\\;1\\le i\\le n.\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  A lower bound of \\(2n-2\\) tiles\n\nConsider the set of squares immediately to the right of the diagonal:\n\n\\[\nS:=\\{(i,i+)\\mid 1\\le i\\le n-1\\}.\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nThe squares of \\(S\\) lie in distinct rows and distinct columns.  \n\n**Claim.** No tile can contain two different squares of \\(S\\).\n\n*Proof of the claim.*  \nSuppose a tile \\(T\\) contains \\((i,i+1)\\) and \\((j,j+1)\\) with \\(i<j\\).  \nSince \\(T\\) is a rectangle, it must also contain the cell \\((j,i+1)\\) (the intersection of row \\(j\\) and column \\(i+1\\)).  \nBecause \\(i+1\\le j\\), the cell \\((j,i+1)\\) lies on or to the left of the diagonal; in particular, when \\(i+1=j\\) it equals the diagonal cell \\((j,j)\\), which is uncovered by (1).  \nThus \\(T\\) would contain an uncovered square, contradicting the definition of a tile. \u220e\n\nConsequently each tile can cover **at most one** element of \\(S\\).  \nSince every square of the region  \n\n\\[\nU:=\\{(i,j)\\mid i<j\\}\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nmust be covered and \\(S\\subseteq U\\), we need at least \\(|S|=n-1\\) tiles to cover \\(U\\).  \n\nBy symmetry, consider the set of squares immediately to the left of the diagonal:\n\n\\[\nT:=\\{(i+1,i)\\mid 1\\le i\\le n-1\\}.\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nExactly the same argument shows that each tile can contain at most one element of \\(T\\), and that at least \\(|T|=n-1\\) tiles are required to cover the lower region  \n\n\\[\nL:=\\{(i,j)\\mid i>j\\}.\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nSince the uncovered squares (1) are not part of \\(U\\cup L\\), any tiling of the whole board must use at least  \n\n\\[\n(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\ntiles.  This establishes the lower bound.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Construction using exactly \\(2n-2\\) tiles\n\nFor each row \\(i\\) with \\(1\\le i\\le n-1\\) define the **right\u2011segment tile**\n\n\\[\nR_i:=\\{(i,j)\\mid i+1\\le j\\le n\\}.\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nFor each row \\(i\\) with \\(2\\le i\\le n\\) define the **left\u2011segment tile**\n\n\\[\nL_i:=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le j\\le i-1\\}.\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\n*Properties of the tiles.*\n\n1. **Disjointness.**  \n   All tiles \\(R_i\\) lie in distinct rows, and all tiles \\(L_i\\) also lie in distinct rows; moreover a tile \\(R_i\\) occupies columns \\(i+1,\\dots ,n\\) while a tile \\(L_i\\) occupies columns \\(1,\\dots ,i-1\\). Hence no two tiles share a unit square.\n\n2. **Avoidance of uncovered squares.**  \n   By (1) the only uncovered squares are \\((i,i)\\).  \n   The tile \\(R_i\\) contains only squares with column index \\(>i\\), so it never contains \\((i,i)\\).  \n   The tile \\(L_i\\) contains only squares with column index \\(<i\\), so it never contains \\((i,i)\\).\n\n3. **Coverage.**  \n   For a fixed row \\(i\\):\n   - If \\(i=1\\), the row consists of the uncovered square \\((1,1)\\) and the right segment \\(R_1\\); thus the row is fully covered except for \\((1,1)\\).\n   - If \\(2\\le i\\le n-1\\), the row consists of the left segment \\(L_i\\), the uncovered square \\((i,i)\\), and the right segment \\(R_i\\); both segments are covered by the corresponding tiles.\n   - If \\(i=n\\), the row consists of the left segment \\(L_n\\) and the uncovered square \\((n,n)\\); the left segment is covered by \\(L_n\\).\n\n   Hence every unit square not on the diagonal is covered by exactly one tile.\n\nThe total number of tiles used is  \n\n\\[\n|\\{R_i\\mid 1\\le i\\le n-1\\}|+|\\{L_i\\mid 2\\le i\\le n\\}|=(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2.\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nThus a tiling with \\(2n-2\\) tiles exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Minimality\n\nThe lower bound (6) shows that no tiling can use fewer than \\(2n-2\\) tiles, while the construction above achieves exactly \\(2n-2\\) tiles.  Therefore the minimum possible number of tiles is \\(2n-2\\).\n\nFor the given board with \\(n=2025\\),\n\n\\[\n2n-2 = 2\\cdot2025-2 = 4048.\n\\]\n\nHence the answer is \\(\\boxed{4048}\\).\n\n---\n\n### Self-Correction Instruction ###\n\nBefore finalizing, verify that:\n\n1. All mathematical statements are enclosed in TeX delimiters.\n2. The lower\u2011bound argument correctly shows that each rectangle can contain at most one cell of the sets \\(S\\) and \\(T\\).\n3. The construction tiles \\(R_i\\) and \\(L_i\\) are disjoint, avoid the diagonal squares, and together cover every non\u2011diagonal square.\n4. The final answer is presented as \\(\\boxed{4048}\\) and the summary follows the required format."
